 & =36(1-0.8) \\\\  & =7.2 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =31 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =31(0.12) \\\\  & =3.72 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =59, \\\\ 5\\%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 500 \\\\  & =25 \\\\  & <59.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5\\%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =36 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =36(0.8) \\\\  & =28.8 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =36(1-0.8) \\\\  & =7.2 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =31 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =31(0.12) \\\\  & =3.72 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\wid