ortion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =15(0.2) \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & ≯5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100(0.96) \\\\  & =96 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100(1-0.96) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =70(0.6) \\\\  & =42 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =70(1-0.6) \\\\  & =28 \\\\  & >5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =15(0.2) \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & ≯5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100(0.96) \\\\  & =96 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100(1-0.96) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =70(0.6) \\\\  & =42 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =70(1-0.6) \\\\  & =28 \\\\  & >5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186634642 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=35,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.65.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=35(0.65)=22.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=35(1-0.65)=12.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=35$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.65,\\frac{0.65(1-0.65)}{35}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.65,0.0065).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $14$ bottles contain less than one liter of soda, then \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}<\\frac{14}{35}=0.4.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.4) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.4-0.65}{\\sqrt{0.0065}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-3.1) \\\\  & =Φ(-3.1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=35,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.65.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=35(0.65)=22.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=35(1-0.65)=12.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=35$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\left(0.65,\\frac{0.65(1-0.65)}{35}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(0.65,0.0065).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $14$ bottles contain less than one liter of soda, then \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}<\\frac{14}{35}=0.4.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.4) & =P\\left(Z<\\frac{0.4-0.65}{\\sqrt{0.0065}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-3.1) \\\\  & =Φ(-3.1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186634644 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{-\\pi n/2}=\n$$" "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(1+\\frac{x}{n}\\right)}^{-\\pi n/2}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186634644 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{x}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{-\\pi n/2} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{-\\pi /2} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{-\\pi /2} \\\\  & ={({e}^{x})}^{-\\pi /2} \\\\  & ={({e}^{\\pi x})}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{\\pi x}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{x}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(1+\\frac{x}{n}\\right)}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(1+\\frac{x}{n}\\right)}^{-\\pi n/2} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left[{\\left(1+\\frac{x}{n}\\right)}^{n}\\right]}^{-\\pi /2} \\\\  & ={\\left[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(1+\\frac{x}{n}\\right)}^{n}\\right]}^{-\\pi /2} \\\\  & ={({e}^{x})}^{-\\pi /2} \\\\  & ={({e}^{\\pi x})}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{\\pi x}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186634665 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{4n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{n}\\right)}^{4n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186634665 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{4n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{4} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{4} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{n}\\right)}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits,