ize),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 2\\,000 \\\\  & =100\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.15) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.15) \\\\  & =34 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40, \\\\ 5\\%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 200 \\\\  & =10 \\\\  & <40.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5\\%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.05) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 2\\,000 \\\\  & =100\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.15) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.15) \\\\  & =34 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40, \\\\ 5\\%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 200 \\\\  & =10 \\\\  & <40.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5\\%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.05) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186634674 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=100,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{120\\,000}{200\\,000}=0.6.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=200(0.6)=120>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=200(1-0.6)=80>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=200$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.6,\\frac{0.6(1-0.6)}{200}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.6,0.0012).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $100$ flights were international, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & <\\frac{100}{200}=0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.5) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{0.5-0.6}{\\sqrt{0.0012}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -2.89) \\\\  & =Φ(-2.89)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-2.89) &=0.0019.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.5) & =Φ(-2.89) \\\\  & =0.0019.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=100,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{120\\,000}{200\\,000}=0.6.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=200(0.6)=120>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=200(1-0.6)=80>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=200$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\left(0.6,\\frac{0.6(1-0.6)}{200}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(0.6,0.0012).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $100$ flights were international, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & <\\frac{100}{200}=0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.5) & =P\\left(Z\\le \\frac{0.5-0.6}{\\sqrt{0.0012}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -2.89) \\\\  & =Φ(-2.89)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-2.89) &=0.0019.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.5) & =Φ(-2.89) \\\\  & =0.0019.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186634677 :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $32$ customers at a clothing store, where $60\\%$ of the customers stay at the store for at least $25$ minutes.\n\nII. A sample of $20$ oranges in the supermarket, where $80\\%$ of the oranges weigh more than $130\\,\\text{g}.$\n\nIII. A sample of $50$ newborns in a town, given that $40\\%$ of the newborns in the country weigh more than $21$ inches long.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $32$ customers at a clothing store, where $60\\%$ of the customers stay at the store for at least $25$ minutes.\n\nII. A sample of $20$ oranges in the supermarket, where $80\\%$ of the oranges weigh more than $130\\,\\text{g}.$\n\nIII. A sample of $50$ newborns in a town, given that $40\\%$ of the newborns in the country weigh more than $21$ inches long.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [:db/cas 17592186634677 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =32(0.6) \\\\  & =19.2 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =32(1-0.6) \\\\  & =12.8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =20(0.8) \\\\  & =16 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =20(1-0.8) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we cannot approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50(0.4) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50(1-0.4) \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =32(0.6) \\\\  & =19.2 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =32(1-0.6) \\\\  & =12.8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =20(0.8) \\\\  & =16 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =20(1-0.8) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we cannot approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50(0.4) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50(1-0.4) \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186634683 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=75,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{2\\,000}{8\\,000}=0.25.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=75(0.25)=18.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=75(1-0.25)=56.25>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=75$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.25,\\frac{0.25(1-0.25)}{75}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.25,0.0025).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $30$ items in the sample are magazines, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & >\\frac{30}{75}=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.4-0.25}{\\sqrt{0.0025}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>3) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-Φ(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(3.00) &=0.9987.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.4) & =1-Φ(3.00) \\\\  & =1-0.9987 \\\\  & =0.0013.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=75,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{2\\,000}{8\\,000}=0.25.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=75(0.25)=18.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=75(1-0.25)=56.25>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=75$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\left(0.25,\\frac{0.25(1-0.25)}{75}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(0.25,0.0025).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $30$ items in the sample are magazines, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & >\\frac{30}{75}=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.4) & =P\\left(Z>\\frac{0.4-0.25}{\\sqrt{0.0025}}\\right) \\\\  & =P(Z>3) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-Φ(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(3.00) &=0.9987.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.4) & =1-Φ(3.00) \\\\  & =1-0.9987 \\\\  & =0.0013.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186634854 :answer/value "r(1)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{11}{2}⟩" "r(1)=\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{11}{2}\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186635100 :answer/value "r(1)=⟨\\frac{13}{20},16⟩" "r(1)=\\left\\langle \\frac{13}{20},16\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186635125 :answer/value "r(1)=⟨\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{20}{3}⟩" "r(1)=\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{20}{3}\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186635265 :answer/value "r(1)=⟨\\frac{2}{5},-16⟩" "r(1)=\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{5},-16\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186635303 :answer/value "r(1)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},16⟩" "r(1)=\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2},16\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186635481 :question/problem "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨-2,\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t+4}}⟩,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(1,6)$ when $t=0,$ calculate the components of the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$r(t)=⟨\\,1-2t,\\,$\n{{field-1}}$⟩$" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=\\left\\langle -2,\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t+4}}\\right\\rangle ,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(1,6)$ when $t=0,$ calculate the components of the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$r(t)=⟨\\,1-2t,\\,$\n{{field-1}}$⟩$"] [:db/cas 17592186635481 :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨-2,\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t+4}}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (-2)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t+4}}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t+{C}_{1},\\,6\\sqrt{t+4}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨1,6⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨-2(0)+{C}_{1},\\,6\\sqrt{0+4}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,6⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},12+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=-6.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨1-2t,\\,6\\sqrt{t+4}-6⟩.\n$$" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\left\\langle -2,\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t+4}}\\right\\rangle \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\int (-2)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t+4}}\\,\\text{d}t\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-2t+{C}_{1},\\,6\\sqrt{t+4}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨1,6⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨-2(0)+{C}_{1},\\,6\\sqrt{0+4}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,6⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},12+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=-6.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨1-2t,\\,6\\sqrt{t+4}-6⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186635484 :question/problem "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2,\\frac{1}{t-3}⟩\\,,\\,t\\in (3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩$ when $t=5,$ calculate the times when the particle is at the point $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3).$" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=\\left\\langle 2,\\frac{1}{t-3}\\right\\rangle \\,,\\,t\\in (3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩$ when $t=5,$ calculate the times when the particle is at the point $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186635484 :question/worked-solution "The particle is at the point $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)$ when its position is $r=⟨4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3⟩.$ So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2,\\frac{1}{t-3}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\frac{1}{t-3}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t+{C}_{1},\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(5)=⟨2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.$ Substituting $t=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(5) & =⟨2(5)+{C}_{1},\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5-3)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩ & =⟨10+{C}_{1},\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-8$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2t-8,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is at the point $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3),$ we solve the equation $r(t)=⟨4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3⟩.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-8,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)⟩ & =⟨4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-8=4 &  & ⇒ &  & t=6, \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 &  & ⇒ &  & t=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution that satisfies both equations is $t=6.$" "The particle is at the point $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)$ when its position is $r=⟨4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3⟩.$ So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\left\\langle 2,\\frac{1}{t-3}\\right\\rangle \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\int 2\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\frac{1}{t-3}\\,\\text{d}t\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2t+{C}_{1},\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(5)=⟨2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.$ Substituting $t=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(5) & =⟨2(5)+{C}_{1},\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5-3)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩ & =⟨10+{C}_{1},\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-8$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2t-8,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is at the point $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3),$ we solve the equation $r(t)=⟨4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3⟩.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-8,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)⟩ & =⟨4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-8=4 &  & ⇒ &  & t=6, \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 &  & ⇒ &  & t=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution that satisfies both equations is $t=6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186635503 :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x$, we have\n\n$$\n4{\\cos }^{2}⁡3x=2(2{\\cos }^{2}⁡3x)=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+1)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4{\\cos }^{2}⁡3x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+2-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+2x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )+2\\pi -\\frac{1}{3}{\\pi }^{3}]-[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+0-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}] \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi -{\\pi }^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (6-{\\pi }^{2})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x$, we have\n\n$$\n4{\\cos }^{2}⁡3x=2(2{\\cos }^{2}⁡3x)=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+1)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4{\\cos }^{2}⁡3x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+2-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{2}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+2x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\right)|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )+2\\pi -\\frac{1}{3}{\\pi }^{3}\\right]-\\left[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+0-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}\\right] \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi -{\\pi }^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (6-{\\pi }^{2})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186635508 :question/problem "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨4{t}^{2}+2t,\\,6{e}^{3t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,2⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=1.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$r(1)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨4{t}^{2}+2t,\\,6{e}^{3t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{3},\\,2\\right\\rangle $ when $t=0,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=1.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$r(1)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$"] [:db/cas 17592186635508 :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨4{t}^{2}+2t,\\,6{e}^{3t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (4{t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 6{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{3t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨\\frac{2}{3},2⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\frac{4}{3}(0)^{3}+(0)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{3(0)}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\frac{2}{3},2⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,2+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3},\\,2{e}^{3t}⟩.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨\\frac{4}{3}(1)^{3}+(1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{3},\\,2{e}^{3(1)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{3}+1+\\frac{2}{3},\\,2{e}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,2{e}^{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨4{t}^{2}+2t,\\,6{e}^{3t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (4{t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 6{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{3t}+{C}_{2}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{3},2\\right\\rangle .$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{4}{3}(0)^{3}+(0)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{3(0)}+{C}_{2}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ \\left\\langle \\frac{2}{3},2\\right\\rangle  & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,2+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\left\\langle \\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3},\\,2{e}^{3t}\\right\\rangle .\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{4}{3}(1)^{3}+(1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{3},\\,2{e}^{3(1)}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{4}{3}+1+\\frac{2}{3},\\,2{e}^{3}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,2{e}^{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186635528 :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =0 \\\\ 3t & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. \n\nTherefore, the extrema of the velocity function, for $t>0,$ are\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6},…\n$$\n\nWe now test the extrema values, starting with the least, using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =-24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-24\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-24<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\nTherefore, the first time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+C & =2 \\\\ 0+C & =2\\,⟹\\,C=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2+\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{\\text{max}} & =v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{14}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{s}.$" "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =0 \\\\ 3t & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. \n\nTherefore, the extrema of the velocity function, for $t>0,$ are\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6},…\n$$\n\nWe now test the extrema values, starting with the least, using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =-24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-24\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-24<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\nTherefore, the first time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+C & =2 \\\\ 0+C & =2\\,⟹\\,C=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2+\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{\\text{max}} & =v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{14}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186635532 :question/worked-solution "From the equations, the direction vectors of the lines are\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=⟨-2,-3,1⟩\\,\\text{and}\\,{v}_{2}=⟨4,6,-2⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{2}=-2\\cdot {v}_{1}.$ This means that ${v}_{1}\\,∥\\,{v}_{2}.$ Therefore, the lines are parallel. \n\nTo find the shortest distance between the lines, we pick a point that lies on one line and compute the shortest distance from it to the other line.\n\nLet's take the point $P(2,-2,1)$ that lies on ${l}_{1}.$ Our goal is to compute the shortest distance between $P$ and ${l}_{2}.$\n\nWriting the equation of ${l}_{2}$ in vector form, we obtain \n\n$$\nr=⟨3+4t,2+6t,1-2t⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe coordinates of any point $Q$ that lies on ${l}_{2}$ can be represented as $(3+4t,2+6t,1-2t)$ for some particular $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨3+4t,2+6t,1-2t⟩-⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+4t,4+6t,-2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ must be perpendicular to ${v}_{2},$ we must have $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, we compute their dot product, set it equal to zero and then solve the resulting equation for $t,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =0 \\\\ ⟨1+4t,4+6t,-2t⟩\\cdot ⟨4,6,-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ (1+4t)\\cdot 4+(4+6t)\\cdot 6+(-2t)\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ 4+16t+24+36t+4t & =0 \\\\ 28+56t & =0 \\\\ 56t & =-28 \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into the expression for $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1+4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right),4+6\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right),-2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the shortest distance between the lines is $\\sqrt{3}.$" "From the equations, the direction vectors of the lines are\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=⟨-2,-3,1⟩\\,\\text{and}\\,{v}_{2}=⟨4,6,-2⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{2}=-2\\cdot {v}_{1}.$ This means that ${v}_{1}\\,∥\\,{v}_{2}.$ Therefore, the lines are parallel. \n\nTo find the shortest distance between the lines, we pick a point that lies on one line and compute the shortest distance from it to the other line.\n\nLet's take the point $P(2,-2,1)$ that lies on ${l}_{1}.$ Our goal is to compute the shortest distance between $P$ and ${l}_{2}.$\n\nWriting the equation of ${l}_{2}$ in vector form, we obtain \n\n$$\nr=⟨3+4t,2+6t,1-2t⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe coordinates of any point $Q$ that lies on ${l}_{2}$ can be represented as $(3+4t,2+6t,1-2t)$ for some particular $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨3+4t,2+6t,1-2t⟩-⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+4t,4+6t,-2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ must be perpendicular to ${v}_{2},$ we must have $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, we compute their dot product, set it equal to zero and then solve the resulting equation for $t,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =0 \\\\ ⟨1+4t,4+6t,-2t⟩\\cdot ⟨4,6,-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ (1+4t)\\cdot 4+(4+6t)\\cdot 6+(-2t)\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ 4+16t+24+36t+4t & =0 \\\\ 28+56t & =0 \\\\ 56t & =-28 \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into the expression for $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\left\\langle 1+4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right),4+6\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right),-2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the shortest distance between the lines is $\\sqrt{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186635541 :question/problem "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨-\\frac{2}{(t+4)^{2}},\\,4(3-t)^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},-32⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=1.$" "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=\\left\\langle -\\frac{2}{(t+4)^{2}},\\,4(3-t)^{2}\\right\\rangle .\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2},-32\\right\\rangle $ when $t=0,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186635541 :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨-\\frac{2}{(t+4)^{2}},\\,4(3-t)^{2}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int -\\frac{2}{(t+4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 4(3-t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{t+4}+{C}_{1},\\,-\\frac{4}{3}(3-t)^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},-32⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\frac{2}{0+4}+{C}_{1},\\,-\\frac{4}{3}(3-0)^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\frac{1}{2},-32⟩ & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}+{C}_{1},\\,-36+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=4.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\frac{2}{t+4},\\,-\\frac{4}{3}(3-t)^{3}+4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{t+4},\\,\\frac{4}{3}(t-3)^{3}+4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨\\frac{2}{1+4},\\,\\frac{4}{3}(1-3)^{3}+4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{20}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\left\\langle -\\frac{2}{(t+4)^{2}},\\,4(3-t)^{2}\\right\\rangle \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\int -\\frac{2}{(t+4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 4(3-t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{t+4}+{C}_{1},\\,-\\frac{4}{3}(3-t)^{3}+{C}_{2}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2},-32\\right\\rangle .$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{0+4}+{C}_{1},\\,-\\frac{4}{3}(3-0)^{3}+{C}_{2}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ \\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2},-32\\right\\rangle  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2}+{C}_{1},\\,-36+{C}_{2}\\right\\rangle ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=4.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{t+4},\\,-\\frac{4}{3}(3-t)^{3}+4\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{t+4},\\,\\frac{4}{3}(t-3)^{3}+4\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{1+4},\\,\\frac{4}{3}(1-3)^{3}+4\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{20}{3}\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186635560 :question/worked-solution "Here, the variable limit $x$ appears as the lower limit of integration. To apply FTC II directly, we prefer the variable on the upper limit. Using the property of definite integrals, we rewrite\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{x}^{1}\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{1}\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, the variable limit $x$ appears as the lower limit of integration. To apply FTC II directly, we prefer the variable on the upper limit. Using the property of definite integrals, we rewrite\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{x}^{1}\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{1}\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-{\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186635569 :question/problem "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨-3{e}^{t},\\,4{e}^{-2t}⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨-3,\\,-2⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the time when the particle is at the point $(-3e,\\,-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe particle is at the point $(-3e,\\,-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}})$ when $t=$\n{{field-1}}." "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨-3{e}^{t},\\,4{e}^{-2t}⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨-3,\\,-2⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the time when the particle is at the point $\\left(-3e,\\,-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}\\right).$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe particle is at the point $\\left(-3e,\\,-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}\\right)$ when $t=$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186635569 :question/worked-solution "The particle is at the point $(-3e,\\,-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}})$ when its position is $r=⟨-3e,\\,-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}⟩.$ So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨-3{e}^{t},\\,4{e}^{-2t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int -3{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{e}^{t}+{C}_{1},\\,-2{e}^{-2t}+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨-3,-2⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨-3{e}^{0}+{C}_{1},\\,-2{e}^{0}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-3,-2⟩ & =⟨-3+{C}_{1},\\,-2+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨-3{e}^{t},\\,-2{e}^{-2t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the time at which the particle is at the point $(-3e,\\,-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}),$ we solve the equation $r(t)=⟨-3e,\\,-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-3{e}^{t},\\,-2{e}^{-2t}⟩ & =⟨-3e,\\,-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{t}=-3e &  & ⇒ &  & t=1, \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t}=-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}} &  & ⇒ &  & t=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution that satisfies both equations is $t=1.$" "The particle is at the point $\\left(-3e,\\,-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}\\right)$ when its position is $r=\\left\\langle -3e,\\,-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}\\right\\rangle .$ So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨-3{e}^{t},\\,4{e}^{-2t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int -3{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{e}^{t}+{C}_{1},\\,-2{e}^{-2t}+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨-3,-2⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨-3{e}^{0}+{C}_{1},\\,-2{e}^{0}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-3,-2⟩ & =⟨-3+{C}_{1},\\,-2+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨-3{e}^{t},\\,-2{e}^{-2t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the time at which the particle is at the point $\\left(-3e,\\,-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}\\right),$ we solve the equation $r(t)=\\left\\langle -3e,\\,-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}\\right\\rangle :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-3{e}^{t},\\,-2{e}^{-2t}⟩ & =\\left\\langle -3e,\\,-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{t}=-3e &  & ⇒ &  & t=1, \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t}=-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}} &  & ⇒ &  & t=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution that satisfies both equations is $t=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186636430 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=5{x}^{3}-19{x}^{2}+21x+5.$ Since $x=-\\frac{1}{5}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+\\frac{1}{5})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+\\frac{1}{5})$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{-\\frac{1}{5}} & 5 & -19 & 21 & 5 \\\\  &  & -1 & 4 & -5 \\\\  & 5 & -20 & 25 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{5})(5{x}^{2}-20x+25).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $5{x}^{2}-20x+25=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-20x+25 & =0 \\\\ 5({x}^{2}-4x+5) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+5 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =(\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-\\frac{1}{5},\\,2\\pm \\text{i}.$" "Let $f(x)=5{x}^{3}-19{x}^{2}+21x+5.$ Since $x=-\\frac{1}{5}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $\\left(x+\\frac{1}{5}\\right)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $\\left(x+\\frac{1}{5}\\right)$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{-\\frac{1}{5}} & 5 & -19 & 21 & 5 \\\\  &  & -1 & 4 & -5 \\\\  & 5 & -20 & 25 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=\\left(x+\\frac{1}{5}\\right)(5{x}^{2}-20x+25).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $5{x}^{2}-20x+25=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-20x+25 & =0 \\\\ 5({x}^{2}-4x+5) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+5 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =(\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-\\frac{1}{5},\\,2\\pm \\text{i}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186636477 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+1.$ Since $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+\\frac{1}{2})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+\\frac{1}{2})$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{-\\frac{1}{2}} & 4 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\  &  & -2 & 2 & -1 \\\\  & 4 & -4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{2})(4{x}^{2}-4x+2).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $4{x}^{2}-4x+2=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-4x+2 & =0 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\text{i} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $$\n\\displaystyle x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.\n$$" "Let $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+1.$ Since $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $\\left(x+\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $\\left(x+\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{-\\frac{1}{2}} & 4 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\  &  & -2 & 2 & -1 \\\\  & 4 & -4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=\\left(x+\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(4{x}^{2}-4x+2).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $4{x}^{2}-4x+2=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-4x+2 & =0 \\\\ 4\\left({x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ {\\left(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ {\\left(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {\\left(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\text{i} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $$\n\\displaystyle x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636666 :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{1}{4}\\overset{\\to }{c},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\overset{\\to }{c} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ⟨4,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4,\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute $\\frac{1}{4}\\overset{\\to }{c},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\overset{\\to }{c} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ⟨4,-4⟩ \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4,\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-4)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637233 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡({\\frac{3\\pi }{2}}^{∘})$ using a calculator." "Evaluate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left({\\frac{3\\pi }{2}}^{∘}\\right)$ using a calculator."] [:db/cas 17592186637233 :question/worked-solution "Recall that we always round to $3$ decimal places unless stated otherwise. \n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there is a \"degree sign\" $({}^{∘})$ next to the $\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ we're required to evaluate this trigonometric expression using degrees. So, make sure that your calculator is in $\\text{DEG}$ (degrees) mode.\n\n- On scientific calculators, you can usually access degrees mode by pressing $\\text{DRG}$ or $\\text{MODE}$ and selecting $\\text{DEG}$ from the menu. You may also need to press $\\text{2nd}$ or $\\text{SHIFT}$ to access the menu.\n\n- On graphing calculators, you can usually access degrees mode by pressing $\\text{MODE}$ and selecting $\\text{DEGREE}$ from the menu.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡({\\frac{3\\pi }{2}}^{∘})≈12.131\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Recall that we always round to $3$ decimal places unless stated otherwise. \n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there is a \"degree sign\" $({}^{∘})$ next to the $\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ we're required to evaluate this trigonometric expression using degrees. So, make sure that your calculator is in $\\text{DEG}$ (degrees) mode.\n\n- On scientific calculators, you can usually access degrees mode by pressing $\\text{DRG}$ or $\\text{MODE}$ and selecting $\\text{DEG}$ from the menu. You may also need to press $\\text{2nd}$ or $\\text{SHIFT}$ to access the menu.\n\n- On graphing calculators, you can usually access degrees mode by pressing $\\text{MODE}$ and selecting $\\text{DEGREE}$ from the menu.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left({\\frac{3\\pi }{2}}^{∘}\\right)≈12.131\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186637251 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =3!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 1!=6\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1=12, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =3+2+1+1+1=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-5\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-5+8+12=15.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =3!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 1!=6\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1=12, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =3+2+1+1+1=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-5\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-5+8+12=15.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637266 :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}(-{\\sec }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,{|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}-(1) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}(-{\\sec }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,{|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\left(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}-(1) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637280 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡({\\frac{\\pi }{3}}^{∘})$ using a calculator." "Evaluate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left({\\frac{\\pi }{3}}^{∘}\\right)$ using a calculator."] [:db/cas 17592186637280 :question/worked-solution "Recall that we always round to $3$ decimal places unless stated otherwise. \n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there is a \"degree sign\" $({}^{∘})$ next to the $\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ we're required to evaluate this trigonometric expression using degrees. So, make sure that your calculator is in $\\text{DEG}$ (degrees) mode.\n\n- On scientific calculators, you can usually access degrees mode by pressing $\\text{DRG}$ or $\\text{MODE}$ and selecting $\\text{DEG}$ from the menu. You may also need to press $\\text{2nd}$ or $\\text{SHIFT}$ to access the menu.\n\n- On graphing calculators, you can usually access degrees mode by pressing $\\text{MODE}$ and selecting $\\text{DEGREE}$ from the menu.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡({\\frac{\\pi }{3}}^{∘})≈0.018\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Recall that we always round to $3$ decimal places unless stated otherwise. \n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there is a \"degree sign\" $({}^{∘})$ next to the $\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ we're required to evaluate this trigonometric expression using degrees. So, make sure that your calculator is in $\\text{DEG}$ (degrees) mode.\n\n- On scientific calculators, you can usually access degrees mode by pressing $\\text{DRG}$ or $\\text{MODE}$ and selecting $\\text{DEG}$ from the menu. You may also need to press $\\text{2nd}$ or $\\text{SHIFT}$ to access the menu.\n\n- On graphing calculators, you can usually access degrees mode by pressing $\\text{MODE}$ and selecting $\\text{DEGREE}$ from the menu.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left({\\frac{\\pi }{3}}^{∘}\\right)≈0.018\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186637293 :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{7}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{7}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\cdot {7}^{x}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}({7}^{0}-{7}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}(1-\\frac{1}{49}) \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{49\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{7}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{7}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\cdot {7}^{x}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}({7}^{0}-{7}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\left(1-\\frac{1}{49}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{49\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637554 :question/worked-solution "First, we use the identity $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}\\frac{9}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}9{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\right)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}9{\\csc }^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}{\\csc }^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=3x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{9}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}9{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}{\\csc }^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =9{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{\\csc }^{2}⁡u\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{\\csc }^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡u{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-3(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =-3(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-1) \\\\  & =3-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we use the identity $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}\\frac{9}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}9{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\right)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}9{\\csc }^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}{\\csc }^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=3x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{9}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}9{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}{\\csc }^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =9{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{\\csc }^{2}⁡u\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{\\csc }^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡u{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-3\\left(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-1\\right) \\\\  & =3-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637565 :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to approximate the value of $\\csc \\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\csc \\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to approximate the value of $\\csc \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4\\right).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\csc \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4\\right)≈$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186637565 :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ f\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\csc \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\csc \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cot \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{\\cot }^{2}⁡x+{\\csc }^{3}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\csc \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right){cot}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{csc}^{3}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =1+(0)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{(1)}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4$ into the above to approximate $\\csc \\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\csc \\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4) & ≈1+\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{4}{25}\\right) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{2}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ f\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\csc \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\csc \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cot \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{\\cot }^{2}⁡x+{\\csc }^{3}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\csc \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right){cot}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{csc}^{3}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =1+(0)\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{(1)}{2!}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4$ into the above to approximate $\\csc \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4\\right).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\csc \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4\\right) & ≈1+\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{4}{25}\\right) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{2}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637594 :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{11}(\\frac{5}{2}n-4)" "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{11}\\left(\\frac{5}{2}n-4\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186637599 :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{11}(\\frac{3}{2}n-3)" "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{11}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}n-3\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186637600 :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}(\\frac{5}{2}n-1)" "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}\\left(\\frac{5}{2}n-1\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186637601 :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{29}(\\frac{5}{2}n-1)" "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{29}\\left(\\frac{5}{2}n-1\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186637602 :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}(\\frac{3}{2}n)" "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}n\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186637606 :question/worked-solution "The first term ${a}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+(n-1)\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}n-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}n-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe don't know the index of the last term, but we can find it using the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =29 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}n-1 & =29 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}n & =29+1 \\\\ n & =30\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ n & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $12$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=12.$ So, the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}(\\frac{5}{2}n-1)\n$$" "The first term ${a}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+(n-1)\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}n-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}n-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe don't know the index of the last term, but we can find it using the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =29 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}n-1 & =29 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}n & =29+1 \\\\ n & =30\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ n & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $12$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=12.$ So, the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}\\left(\\frac{5}{2}n-1\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186638233 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}y-\\frac{1}{x} & x-\\frac{1}{y}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-\\frac{1}{x})=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-\\frac{1}{y})=1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-\\frac{1}{x})=1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-\\frac{1}{y})=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}y-\\frac{1}{x} & x-\\frac{1}{y}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(y-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(x-\\frac{1}{y}\\right)=1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(y-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(x-\\frac{1}{y}\\right)=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186638236 :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.8 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular.\nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq &=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.8 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & -0.3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-0.2 & 0.1 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.2 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & -0.3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}: &=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}: &=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}: &=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-2 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}: &={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3})-3{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{12}{3}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{12} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the $10$ million population will live in towns. Therefore, the total number of people living in towns after many years will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 10\\,\\text{million}=2.5\\,\\text{million}.\n$$" "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.8 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular.\nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq &=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.8 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & -0.3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-0.2 & 0.1 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.2 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & -0.3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}: &=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}: &=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}: &=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-2 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}: &={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+\\left(\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3}\\right)-3{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{12}{3}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{12} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the $10$ million population will live in towns. Therefore, the total number of people living in towns after many years will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 10\\,\\text{million}=2.5\\,\\text{million}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186638264 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}+3).$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}+3\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186638264 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}+3)=\\infty .\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}+3\\right)=\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639195 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $750\\,\\text{L}$ of brine.\n\n- Fresh water enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $25t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n750+20t-25t=(750-5t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nThen, since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{750-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{750-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{150-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =0-\\frac{5Q}{150-t} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{5}{150-t}\\,Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{5}{150-t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{5}{150-t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-5\\int \\frac{1}{150-t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-5(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|150-t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(150-t)^{5}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(150-t)^{5}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A(150-t)^{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $16\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=16,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =16 \\\\ A(150-0)^{5} & =16 \\\\ A & =\\frac{16}{{150}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=16{\\left(\\frac{150-t}{150}\\right)}^{5}\n$$" "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $750\\,\\text{L}$ of brine.\n\n- Fresh water enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $25t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n750+20t-25t=(750-5t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nThen, since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{750-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{750-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{750-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{750-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{150-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =0-\\frac{5Q}{150-t} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{5}{150-t}\\,Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{5}{150-t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{5}{150-t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-5\\int \\frac{1}{150-t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-5(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|150-t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(150-t)^{5}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(150-t)^{5}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A(150-t)^{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $16\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=16,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =16 \\\\ A(150-0)^{5} & =16 \\\\ A & =\\frac{16}{{150}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=16{\\left(\\frac{150-t}{150}\\right)}^{5}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639205 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the mass of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $16\\,\\text{g/L}$ of salt enters the tank at a rate of $7\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(16\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (7\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(16\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (7\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =112\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $900\\,\\text{L}$ of water.\n\n- A solution enters the tank at a rate of $7\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $7t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $9\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $9t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n900+7t-9t=900-2t\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{900-2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $9\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(9\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{900-2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{900-2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9Q}{900-2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =112-\\frac{9Q}{900-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{9}{900-2t}Q &=112.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $Q$ is the mass of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $16\\,\\text{g/L}$ of salt enters the tank at a rate of $7\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(16\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(16\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =112\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $900\\,\\text{L}$ of water.\n\n- A solution enters the tank at a rate of $7\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $7t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $9\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $9t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n900+7t-9t=900-2t\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{900-2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $9\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(9\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{900-2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{900-2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{9Q}{900-2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =112-\\frac{9Q}{900-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{9}{900-2t}Q &=112.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639224 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of sugar in the vat at time $t.$ Then, the derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the vat minus the rate at which sugar leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′},$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the vat.\n\nFirst, note that pure water (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of sugar) enters the vat at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the vat is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the vat.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the vat, we must first determine the volume of sugar-water in the vat at time $t.$\n\nThe vat initially contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; pure water enters at $8\\,\\text{gal/min},$ and mixed solution leaves at $5\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the vat after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\nV(t)=180+8t-5t=3(60+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the vat is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the vat, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(60+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $5\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(5\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(60+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(60+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{3(60+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{5Q}{3(60+t)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{5}{3(60+t)}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{5}{3(60+t)} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{5}{3(60+t)}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{60+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|60+t|+C \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(60+t)^{5/3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nUsing the initial condition $Q(0)=24,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{A}{{60}^{5/3}} & =24 \\\\ A & =24(60)^{5/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nQ(t)=24{\\left(\\frac{60}{60+t}\\right)}^{5/3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the vat begins to overflow. Since the vat has a capacity of $300\\,\\text{gal},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 3(60+{t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 60+{t}_{o} & =100 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =40\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of sugar in the vat at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(40) & =24{\\left(\\frac{60}{60+40}\\right)}^{5/3} \\\\  & =24{\\left(\\frac{6}{10}\\right)}^{5/3} \\\\  & ≈10.24\\,\\text{lb},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "Let $Q$ be the amount of sugar in the vat at time $t.$ Then, the derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the vat minus the rate at which sugar leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′},$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the vat.\n\nFirst, note that pure water (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of sugar) enters the vat at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the vat is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(8\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the vat.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the vat, we must first determine the volume of sugar-water in the vat at time $t.$\n\nThe vat initially contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; pure water enters at $8\\,\\text{gal/min},$ and mixed solution leaves at $5\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the vat after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\nV(t)=180+8t-5t=3(60+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the vat is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the vat, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(60+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $5\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(5\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{3(60+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{3(60+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{3(60+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{5Q}{3(60+t)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{5}{3(60+t)}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{5}{3(60+t)} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{5}{3(60+t)}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{60+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|60+t|+C \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(60+t)^{5/3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nUsing the initial condition $Q(0)=24,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{A}{{60}^{5/3}} & =24 \\\\ A & =24(60)^{5/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nQ(t)=24{\\left(\\frac{60}{60+t}\\right)}^{5/3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the vat begins to overflow. Since the vat has a capacity of $300\\,\\text{gal},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 3(60+{t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 60+{t}_{o} & =100 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =40\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of sugar in the vat at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(40) & =24{\\left(\\frac{60}{60+40}\\right)}^{5/3} \\\\  & =24{\\left(\\frac{6}{10}\\right)}^{5/3} \\\\  & ≈10.24\\,\\text{lb},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186639251 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nFirst, note that pure solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $100\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (100\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (100\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nTo find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\nThe reactor initially contains $8\\,000\\,\\text{mL}$ of solution; pure solvent enters at $100\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed solution leaves at $80\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reactor after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=8\\,000+100t-80t=20(400+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reactor is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{20(400+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $80\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(80\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{20(400+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(80\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{20(400+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{400+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{4Q}{400+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{4}{400+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{4}{400+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{4}{400+t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{400+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|400+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(400+t)^{-4}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(400+t)^{-4}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(400+t)^{4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reactor initially contains $150\\,\\text{g}$ of reactant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=150,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =150 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(400+0)^{4}} & =150 \\\\ A & =150(400)^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{150(400)^{4}}{(400+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =150{\\left(\\frac{400}{400+t}\\right)}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reactor begins to overflow. The reactor has a capacity of $10\\,000\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=10\\,000$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =10\\,000 \\\\ 20(400+{t}_{o}) & =10\\,000 \\\\ 400+{t}_{o} & =500 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =100\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of reactant in the reactor at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(100) & =150{\\left(\\frac{400}{400+100}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =150{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =61.44\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $Q$ be the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nFirst, note that pure solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $100\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(100\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(100\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nTo find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\nThe reactor initially contains $8\\,000\\,\\text{mL}$ of solution; pure solvent enters at $100\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed solution leaves at $80\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reactor after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=8\\,000+100t-80t=20(400+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reactor is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{20(400+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $80\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(80\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{20(400+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(80\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{20(400+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{400+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{4Q}{400+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{4}{400+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{4}{400+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{4}{400+t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{400+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|400+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(400+t)^{-4}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(400+t)^{-4}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(400+t)^{4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reactor initially contains $150\\,\\text{g}$ of reactant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=150,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =150 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(400+0)^{4}} & =150 \\\\ A & =150(400)^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{150(400)^{4}}{(400+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =150{\\left(\\frac{400}{400+t}\\right)}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reactor begins to overflow. The reactor has a capacity of $10\\,000\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=10\\,000$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =10\\,000 \\\\ 20(400+{t}_{o}) & =10\\,000 \\\\ 400+{t}_{o} & =500 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =100\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of reactant in the reactor at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(100) & =150{\\left(\\frac{400}{400+100}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =150{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =61.44\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639256 :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(1/2)}f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\nHowever, the left-sided limit $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(1/2)^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nConsequently, the overall limit does not exist either:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "To compute $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(1/2)}f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\nHowever, the left-sided limit $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(1/2)^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in \\left(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right).$ \n\nConsequently, the overall limit does not exist either:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639571 :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" "t\\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186639598 :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" "t\\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186639972 :question/worked-solution "The variable $c$ is being *divided* by $8.$ To isolate $c$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-8$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-\\frac{c}{8} \\\\ (-8)\\cdot (-7)\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,(-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{c}{8}\\right) \\\\ 56\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,(-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{c}{8}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}56\\,\\, & \\le (-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{c}{8}\\right) \\\\ 56 & \\le (\\cancel{-}8)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{c}{8}) \\\\ 56 & \\le 8\\cdot \\frac{c}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel the common factor $8$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}56 & \\le 8\\cdot \\frac{c}{8} \\\\ 56 & \\le \\frac{8c}{8} \\\\ 56 & \\le \\frac{\\cancel{8}c}{\\cancel{8}} \\\\ 56 & \\le c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the inequality with the variable on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nc\\ge 56\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $c\\ge 56.$" "The variable $c$ is being *divided* by $8.$ To isolate $c$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-8$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-\\frac{c}{8} \\\\ (-8)\\cdot (-7)\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,(-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{c}{8}\\right) \\\\ 56\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,(-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{c}{8}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}56\\,\\, & \\le (-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{c}{8}\\right) \\\\ 56 & \\le (\\cancel{-}8)\\cdot \\left(\\cancel{-}\\frac{c}{8}\\right) \\\\ 56 & \\le 8\\cdot \\frac{c}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel the common factor $8$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}56 & \\le 8\\cdot \\frac{c}{8} \\\\ 56 & \\le \\frac{8c}{8} \\\\ 56 & \\le \\frac{\\cancel{8}c}{\\cancel{8}} \\\\ 56 & \\le c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the inequality with the variable on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nc\\ge 56\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $c\\ge 56.$"] [:db/cas 17592186639974 :question/worked-solution "The variable $v$ is being *divided* by $9.$ To isolate $v$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-9.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{v}{9}\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,5 \\\\ (-9)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{v}{9}\\right)\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,(-9)\\cdot 5 \\\\ (-9)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{v}{9}\\right)\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{v}{9}\\right) & \\le -45 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}9)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{v}{9}) & \\le -45 \\\\ 9\\cdot \\frac{v}{9} & \\le -45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $9$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\cdot \\frac{v}{9} & \\le -45 \\\\ \\frac{9v}{9} & \\le 45 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{9}v}{\\cancel{9}} & \\le -45 \\\\ v & \\le -45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $v\\le -45.$" "The variable $v$ is being *divided* by $9.$ To isolate $v$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-9.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{v}{9}\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,5 \\\\ (-9)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{v}{9}\\right)\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,(-9)\\cdot 5 \\\\ (-9)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{v}{9}\\right)\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{v}{9}\\right) & \\le -45 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}9)\\cdot \\left(\\cancel{-}\\frac{v}{9}\\right) & \\le -45 \\\\ 9\\cdot \\frac{v}{9} & \\le -45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $9$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\cdot \\frac{v}{9} & \\le -45 \\\\ \\frac{9v}{9} & \\le 45 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{9}v}{\\cancel{9}} & \\le -45 \\\\ v & \\le -45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $v\\le -45.$"] [:db/cas 17592186639984 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x){\\sec }^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x){\\sec }^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({2}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x){\\sec }^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x){\\sec }^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left({2}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)}^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(4-\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186640000 :question/worked-solution "The variable $x$ is being *divided* by $2.$ To isolate $x$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-2$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot 3\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,(-2)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{2}\\right) \\\\ -6\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,(-2)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\,\\, & \\ge (-2)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{2}\\right) \\\\ -6 & \\ge (\\cancel{-}2)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\ -6 & \\ge 2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel the common factor $2$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & \\ge 2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} \\\\ -6 & \\ge \\frac{2x}{2} \\\\ -6 & \\ge \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} \\\\ -6 & \\ge x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the inequality with the variable on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx\\le -6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\le -6.$" "The variable $x$ is being *divided* by $2.$ To isolate $x$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-2$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot 3\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,(-2)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{2}\\right) \\\\ -6\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,(-2)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\,\\, & \\ge (-2)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{2}\\right) \\\\ -6 & \\ge (\\cancel{-}2)\\cdot \\left(\\cancel{-}\\frac{x}{2}\\right) \\\\ -6 & \\ge 2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel the common factor $2$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & \\ge 2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} \\\\ -6 & \\ge \\frac{2x}{2} \\\\ -6 & \\ge \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} \\\\ -6 & \\ge x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the inequality with the variable on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx\\le -6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\le -6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186640009 :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between two points where $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula:\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}{x}^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=5.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\sqrt{3x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\sqrt{3x}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{1+[\\sqrt{3x}]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{1+3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=1+3x.$ Then, $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|l|c|}\\hline x & 0 & 5 \\\\ \\hline u & 1 & 16 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{16}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}]|}_{1}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}({16}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(64-1)}{9} \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between two points where $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula:\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}{x}^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=5.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\sqrt{3x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\sqrt{3x}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{1+[\\sqrt{3x}]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{1+3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=1+3x.$ Then, $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|l|c|}\\hline x & 0 & 5 \\\\ \\hline u & 1 & 16 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{16}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\left[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\right]|}_{1}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}({16}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(64-1)}{9} \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186640036 :question/worked-solution "The variable $w$ is being *divided* by $6.$ To isolate $w$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-6.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{w}{6}\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-2 \\\\ (-6)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{w}{6}\\right)\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,(-6)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ (-6)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{w}{6}\\right)\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{w}{6}\\right)\\,\\, & \\le 12 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}6)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{w}{6}) & \\le 12 \\\\ 6\\cdot \\frac{w}{6} & \\le 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $6$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{w}{6} & \\le 12 \\\\ \\frac{6w}{6} & \\le 12 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{6}w}{\\cancel{6}} & \\le 12 \\\\ w & \\le 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $w\\le 12.$" "The variable $w$ is being *divided* by $6.$ To isolate $w$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-6.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{w}{6}\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-2 \\\\ (-6)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{w}{6}\\right)\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,(-6)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ (-6)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{w}{6}\\right)\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{w}{6}\\right)\\,\\, & \\le 12 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}6)\\cdot \\left(\\cancel{-}\\frac{w}{6}\\right) & \\le 12 \\\\ 6\\cdot \\frac{w}{6} & \\le 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $6$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{w}{6} & \\le 12 \\\\ \\frac{6w}{6} & \\le 12 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{6}w}{\\cancel{6}} & \\le 12 \\\\ w & \\le 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $w\\le 12.$"] [:db/cas 17592186640040 :question/worked-solution "The variable $n$ is being *divided* by $7.$ To isolate $n$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-7.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{n}{7}\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,8 \\\\ (-7)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{n}{7}\\right)\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,(-7)\\cdot 8 \\\\ (-7)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{n}{7}\\right)\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{n}{7}\\right)\\,\\, & \\ge -56 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}7)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{n}{7}) & \\ge -56 \\\\ 7\\cdot \\frac{n}{7} & \\ge -56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $7$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\cdot \\frac{n}{7} & \\ge -56 \\\\ \\frac{7n}{7} & \\ge -56 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7}n}{\\cancel{7}} & \\ge -56 \\\\ n & \\ge -56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $n\\ge -56.$" "The variable $n$ is being *divided* by $7.$ To isolate $n$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-7.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{n}{7}\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,8 \\\\ (-7)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{n}{7}\\right)\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,(-7)\\cdot 8 \\\\ (-7)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{n}{7}\\right)\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{n}{7}\\right)\\,\\, & \\ge -56 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}7)\\cdot \\left(\\cancel{-}\\frac{n}{7}\\right) & \\ge -56 \\\\ 7\\cdot \\frac{n}{7} & \\ge -56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $7$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\cdot \\frac{n}{7} & \\ge -56 \\\\ \\frac{7n}{7} & \\ge -56 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7}n}{\\cancel{7}} & \\ge -56 \\\\ n & \\ge -56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $n\\ge -56.$"] [:db/cas 17592186640042 :question/worked-solution "The variable $z$ is being *divided* by $7.$ To isolate $z$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-7.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z}{7}\\,\\, & <\\,\\,4 \\\\ (-7)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{z}{7}\\right)\\,\\, & >\\,\\,(-7)\\cdot 4 \\\\ (-7)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{z}{7}\\right)\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{z}{7}\\right) & >-28 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}7)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{z}{7}) & >-28 \\\\ 7\\cdot \\frac{z}{7} & >-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $7$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\cdot \\frac{z}{7} & >-28 \\\\ \\frac{7z}{7} & >-28 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7}z}{\\cancel{7}} & >-28 \\\\ z & >-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z>-28.$" "The variable $z$ is being *divided* by $7.$ To isolate $z$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-7.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z}{7}\\,\\, & <\\,\\,4 \\\\ (-7)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{z}{7}\\right)\\,\\, & >\\,\\,(-7)\\cdot 4 \\\\ (-7)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{z}{7}\\right)\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{z}{7}\\right) & >-28 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}7)\\cdot \\left(\\cancel{-}\\frac{z}{7}\\right) & >-28 \\\\ 7\\cdot \\frac{z}{7} & >-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $7$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\cdot \\frac{z}{7} & >-28 \\\\ \\frac{7z}{7} & >-28 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7}z}{\\cancel{7}} & >-28 \\\\ z & >-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z>-28.$"] [:db/cas 17592186640046 :question/worked-solution "We use the identities $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}-2) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identities $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\left(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}-2\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186640050 :question/worked-solution "The variable $x$ is being *divided* by $8.$ To isolate $x$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-8.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{x}{8}\\,\\, & >\\,\\,3 \\\\ (-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{8}\\right)\\,\\, & <\\,\\,(-8)\\cdot 3 \\\\ (-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{8}\\right)\\,\\, & <\\,\\,-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{8}\\right) & <-24 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}8)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{x}{8}) & <-24 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{x}{8} & <-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $8$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot \\frac{x}{8} & <-24 \\\\ \\frac{8x}{8} & <-24 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{8}x}{\\cancel{8}} & <-24 \\\\ x & <-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x<-24.$" "The variable $x$ is being *divided* by $8.$ To isolate $x$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-8.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{x}{8}\\,\\, & >\\,\\,3 \\\\ (-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{8}\\right)\\,\\, & <\\,\\,(-8)\\cdot 3 \\\\ (-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{8}\\right)\\,\\, & <\\,\\,-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{8}\\right) & <-24 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}8)\\cdot \\left(\\cancel{-}\\frac{x}{8}\\right) & <-24 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{x}{8} & <-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $8$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot \\frac{x}{8} & <-24 \\\\ \\frac{8x}{8} & <-24 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{8}x}{\\cancel{8}} & <-24 \\\\ x & <-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x<-24.$"] [:db/cas 17592186640238 :answer/value "(x, \\frac{10}{3}x - \\frac{5}{3})" "\\left(x, \\frac{10}{3}x - \\frac{5}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186640239 :answer/value "(x, \\frac{1}{3}x - \\frac{5}{3})" "\\left(x, \\frac{1}{3}x - \\frac{5}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186640241 :answer/value "(x, \\frac{10}{3}x + \\frac{7}{3})" "\\left(x, \\frac{10}{3}x + \\frac{7}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186640242 :answer/value "(x, \\frac{10}{3}x - \\frac{10}{3})" "\\left(x, \\frac{10}{3}x - \\frac{10}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186640243 :answer/value "(x, \\frac{1}{3}x + \\frac{7}{3})" "\\left(x, \\frac{1}{3}x + \\frac{7}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186640252 :answer/value "(6,\\frac{3}{2})" "\\left(6,\\frac{3}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186640255 :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},-18)" "\\left(\\frac{3}{2},-18\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186640258 :answer/value "(-6,-\\frac{9}{2})" "\\left(-6,-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186640260 :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},6)" "\\left(\\frac{3}{2},6\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186640263 :answer/value "(-\\frac{9}{2},6)" "\\left(-\\frac{9}{2},6\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186640268 :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y &=-3 \\\\ 4x-4y &=-42\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then its $x$-term becomes $-4x$, which cancels the $x$-term in the other equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x-2y &=6 \\\\ 4x-4y &=-42\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable and allows us to solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2y-4y) &=(6-42) \\\\ -6y &=-36 \\\\ y &=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=6$ into the second equation $4x-4y=-42$ to solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4(6)+4x &=-42 \\\\ 4x &=-18 \\\\ x &=-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-\\frac{9}{2},\\,y=6$. So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(-\\frac{9}{2},6)$." "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y &=-3 \\\\ 4x-4y &=-42\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then its $x$-term becomes $-4x$, which cancels the $x$-term in the other equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x-2y &=6 \\\\ 4x-4y &=-42\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable and allows us to solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2y-4y) &=(6-42) \\\\ -6y &=-36 \\\\ y &=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=6$ into the second equation $4x-4y=-42$ to solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4(6)+4x &=-42 \\\\ 4x &=-18 \\\\ x &=-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-\\frac{9}{2},\\,y=6$. So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $\\left(-\\frac{9}{2},6\\right)$."] [:db/cas 17592186640270 :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y &=21 \\\\ -4x-6y &=-33\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-3$, then its $y$-term becomes $6y$, which cancels the $y$-term in the other equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12x+6y &=-63 \\\\ -4x-6y &=-33\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-12x-4x) &=(-63-33) \\\\ -16x &=-96 \\\\ x &=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=6$ into the second equation $-4x-6y=-33$ to solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4(6)-6y &=-33 \\\\ -6y &=-9 \\\\ y &=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=6,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}$. So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(6,\\frac{3}{2})$." "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y &=21 \\\\ -4x-6y &=-33\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-3$, then its $y$-term becomes $6y$, which cancels the $y$-term in the other equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12x+6y &=-63 \\\\ -4x-6y &=-33\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-12x-4x) &=(-63-33) \\\\ -16x &=-96 \\\\ x &=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=6$ into the second equation $-4x-6y=-33$ to solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4(6)-6y &=-33 \\\\ -6y &=-9 \\\\ y &=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=6,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}$. So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $\\left(6,\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$."] [:db/cas 17592186640271 :answer/value "(6,\\frac{9}{2})" "\\left(6,\\frac{9}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186640272 :answer/value "(6,\\frac{5}{2})" "\\left(6,\\frac{5}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186640278 :answer/value "(4,\\frac{7}{2})" "\\left(4,\\frac{7}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186640281 :answer/value "(6,-\\frac{9}{2})" "\\left(6,-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186640549 :answer/value "r=⟨-3,2,5⟩+s⟨1,\\frac{2}{3},3⟩+t⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "r=⟨-3,2,5⟩+s\\left\\langle 1,\\frac{2}{3},3\\right\\rangle +t⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186640559 :answer/value "r=⟨1,\\frac{2}{3},3⟩+s⟨-3,2,5⟩+t⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "r=\\left\\langle 1,\\frac{2}{3},3\\right\\rangle +s⟨-3,2,5⟩+t⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186640613 :answer/value "r=⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩+s⟨-3,2,5⟩+t⟨1,\\frac{2}{3},3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "r=⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩+s⟨-3,2,5⟩+t\\left\\langle 1,\\frac{2}{3},3\\right\\rangle ,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186640649 :answer/value "r=⟨1,\\frac{2}{3},3⟩+s⟨-2,5,\\sqrt{6}⟩+t⟨1,-3,0⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "r=\\left\\langle 1,\\frac{2}{3},3\\right\\rangle +s⟨-2,5,\\sqrt{6}⟩+t⟨1,-3,0⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186640764 :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4}{3}t,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{3}+2)=3{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{4}{3}t)}{3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}{3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=3,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,3}=\\frac{4}{9({3}^{2})}=\\frac{4}{81}.\n$$" "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4}{3}t,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{3}+2)=3{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}t\\right)}{3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}{3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=3,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,3}=\\frac{4}{9({3}^{2})}=\\frac{4}{81}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186640769 :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(1,\\frac{2}{3},3)$ and is parallel to the lines $\\mathbf{r}=⟨-2,5,\\sqrt{6}⟩+s⟨-3,2,5⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨1,-3,0⟩+t⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩.$" "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q\\left(1,\\frac{2}{3},3\\right)$ and is parallel to the lines $\\mathbf{r}=⟨-2,5,\\sqrt{6}⟩+s⟨-3,2,5⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨1,-3,0⟩+t⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186640769 :question/worked-solution "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨-3,2,5⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $Q(1,\\frac{2}{3},3)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $q=⟨1,\\frac{2}{3},3⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =q+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨1,\\frac{2}{3},3⟩+s⟨-3,2,5⟩+t⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨-3,2,5⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $Q\\left(1,\\frac{2}{3},3\\right)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $q=\\left\\langle 1,\\frac{2}{3},3\\right\\rangle .$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =q+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =\\left\\langle 1,\\frac{2}{3},3\\right\\rangle +s⟨-3,2,5⟩+t⟨1,20,\\sqrt{5}⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641599 :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨0,5,-2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-2z+15 & =0 \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{2}y+\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $y=0$, we get $z=\\frac{15}{2}.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,\\frac{15}{2})$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,\\frac{15}{2}⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,\\frac{15}{2}⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,5,-2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨0,5,-2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-2z+15 & =0 \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{2}y+\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $y=0$, we get $z=\\frac{15}{2}.$ Hence, the point $P\\left(0,0,\\frac{15}{2}\\right)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=\\left\\langle 0,0,\\frac{15}{2}\\right\\rangle .$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ \\left(r-\\left\\langle 0,0,\\frac{15}{2}\\right\\rangle \\right)\\cdot ⟨0,5,-2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641617 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the factor $5$ outside the summation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}5{n}^{3}=5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}{n}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{N}^{2}(N+1)^{2}\n$$\n\nwith $N=10$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}{n}^{3} & =5(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {10}^{2}(10+1)^{2}) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {10}^{2}\\cdot {11}^{2}) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 100\\cdot 121) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 3\\,025 \\\\  & =15\\,125.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we take the factor $5$ outside the summation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}5{n}^{3}=5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}{n}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{N}^{2}(N+1)^{2}\n$$\n\nwith $N=10$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}{n}^{3} & =5\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {10}^{2}(10+1)^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {10}^{2}\\cdot {11}^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 100\\cdot 121\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 3\\,025 \\\\  & =15\\,125.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641627 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186641627 :question/worked-solution "From the identity $1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta$ with $\\theta =2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})={\\cos }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the identity $1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta$ with $\\theta =2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(2\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641638 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a unique vector $∇f$ such that the following equality is true: \n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot ∇f(x)+o(h)\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by computing the left-hand side of the equation. Setting $h={h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j$, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =(\\frac{1}{2}(\\,(x+{h}_{1})^{2}+(y+{h}_{2})^{2})+\\frac{1}{4})-(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+2x{h}_{1}+{h}_{1}^{2})+\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{2}+2y{h}_{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})\\,+\\frac{1}{4})-(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4})+x{h}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{h}_{1}^{2}+y{h}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{h}_{2}^{2}-(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =x{h}_{1}+y{h}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x{h}_{1}+y{h}_{2}$ is linear with respect to ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}$, we can further rewrite our expression using the dot product, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =(x{h}_{1}+y{h}_{2})+\\frac{1}{2}({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =(x\\,i+y\\,j)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j)+\\frac{1}{2}({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =h\\cdot (x\\,i+y\\,j)+\\frac{1}{2}‖h{‖}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that $\\frac{1}{2}‖h{‖}^{2}=o(h),$ because \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\,\\frac{1}{2}‖h{‖}^{2}\\,}{||h||} &=\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{h\\to 0}‖h‖=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot (x\\,i+y\\,j)+o(h).\n$$\n\nFinally, comparing the expression above with the definition of $∇f,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n∇f=x\\,i+y\\,j.\n$$" "We need to find a unique vector $∇f$ such that the following equality is true: \n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot ∇f(x)+o(h)\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by computing the left-hand side of the equation. Setting $h={h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j$, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2}(\\,(x+{h}_{1})^{2}+(y+{h}_{2})^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+2x{h}_{1}+{h}_{1}^{2})+\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{2}+2y{h}_{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})\\,+\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+x{h}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{h}_{1}^{2}+y{h}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{h}_{2}^{2}-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =x{h}_{1}+y{h}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x{h}_{1}+y{h}_{2}$ is linear with respect to ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}$, we can further rewrite our expression using the dot product, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+h)-f(x) & =(x{h}_{1}+y{h}_{2})+\\frac{1}{2}({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =(x\\,i+y\\,j)\\cdot ({h}_{1}\\,i+{h}_{2}\\,j)+\\frac{1}{2}({h}_{1}^{2}+{h}_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =h\\cdot (x\\,i+y\\,j)+\\frac{1}{2}‖h{‖}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that $\\frac{1}{2}‖h{‖}^{2}=o(h),$ because \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\,\\frac{1}{2}‖h{‖}^{2}\\,}{||h||} &=\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{h\\to 0}‖h‖=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x+h)-f(x)=h\\cdot (x\\,i+y\\,j)+o(h).\n$$\n\nFinally, comparing the expression above with the definition of $∇f,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n∇f=x\\,i+y\\,j.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641641 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have to use the identity\n\n$$\n1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x={\\csc }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{{\\cot }^{2}⁡x-{\\csc }^{2}⁡x}{{\\csc }^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}-{\\cot }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{{\\cot }^{2}⁡x-(1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x)}{(1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x)+{x}^{2}-{\\cot }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{{\\cot }^{2}⁡x-1-{\\cot }^{2}⁡x}{1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}-{\\cot }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{(-1)}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,{|}_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{2\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, we have to use the identity\n\n$$\n1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x={\\csc }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{{\\cot }^{2}⁡x-{\\csc }^{2}⁡x}{{\\csc }^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}-{\\cot }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{{\\cot }^{2}⁡x-(1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x)}{(1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x)+{x}^{2}-{\\cot }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{{\\cot }^{2}⁡x-1-{\\cot }^{2}⁡x}{1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}-{\\cot }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{(-1)}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,{|}_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =-\\left(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641652 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formulas:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{N}^{2}(N+1)^{2}=({\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(4{n}^{3}-3{n}^{2}) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}4{n}^{3}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}3{n}^{2} \\\\  & =4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}{n}^{3}-3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}{n}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the two series separately.  For the first series we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}{n}^{3} & =4(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {22}^{2}(22+1)^{2}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {22}^{2}\\cdot {23}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\cancel{4}(\\frac{1}{\\cancel{4}}\\cdot {22}^{2}\\cdot {23}^{2}) \\\\  & =256\\,036.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second series, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}{n}^{2} & =3(\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 22(22+1)(2\\cdot 22+1)) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 22\\cdot 23\\cdot 45) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3\\,795 \\\\  & =11\\,385.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(4{n}^{3}-3{n}^{2}) & =256\\,036-11\\,385 \\\\  & =244\\,651.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we recall the following formulas:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{N}^{2}(N+1)^{2}=({\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(4{n}^{3}-3{n}^{2}) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}4{n}^{3}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}3{n}^{2} \\\\  & =4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}{n}^{3}-3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}{n}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the two series separately.  For the first series we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}{n}^{3} & =4\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {22}^{2}(22+1)^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {22}^{2}\\cdot {23}^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\cancel{4}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\cancel{4}}\\cdot {22}^{2}\\cdot {23}^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =256\\,036.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second series, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}{n}^{2} & =3\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 22(22+1)(2\\cdot 22+1)\\right) \\\\  & =3\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 22\\cdot 23\\cdot 45\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3\\,795 \\\\  & =11\\,385.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(4{n}^{3}-3{n}^{2}) & =256\\,036-11\\,385 \\\\  & =244\\,651.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641659 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the factor $-3$ outside the summation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{11}-3{n}^{3}=-3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{11}{n}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{N}^{2}(N+1)^{2}\n$$\n\nwith $N=11$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{11}{n}^{3} & =-3(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {11}^{2}(11+1)^{2}) \\\\  & =-3(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {11}^{2}\\cdot {12}^{2}) \\\\  & =-3(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 121\\cdot 144) \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4\\,356 \\\\  & =-13\\,068.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we take the factor $-3$ outside the summation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{11}-3{n}^{3}=-3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{11}{n}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{N}^{2}(N+1)^{2}\n$$\n\nwith $N=11$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{11}{n}^{3} & =-3\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {11}^{2}(11+1)^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {11}^{2}\\cdot {12}^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 121\\cdot 144\\right) \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4\\,356 \\\\  & =-13\\,068.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641795 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(-1)\\cdot\\left(\\frac{8}{(x-3)^{2}}\\right).\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =(-1)\\cdot(\\int \\frac{8}{(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x )\\\\ y & =(-1)\\cdot(8\\int \\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x )\\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot(8\\left(-\\frac{1}{x-3}\\right))+C \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{8}{x-3}\\right)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y=(-1)\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{8}{x-3}\\right)+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(5)=3$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =\\frac{8}{(5) - 3}+C \\\\ 3 & =4+C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-1 + \\frac{8}{x - 3}.\n$$" "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(-1)\\cdot\\left(\\frac{8}{(x-3)^{2}}\\right).\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =(-1)\\cdot\\left(\\int \\frac{8}{(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\right)\\\\ y & =(-1)\\cdot\\left(8\\int \\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\right)\\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot\\left(8\\left(-\\frac{1}{x-3}\\right)\\right)+C \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{8}{x-3}\\right)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y=(-1)\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{8}{x-3}\\right)+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(5)=3$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =\\frac{8}{(5) - 3}+C \\\\ 3 & =4+C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-1 + \\frac{8}{x - 3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641796 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(4)\\cdot(6{x}^{2}).\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =(4)\\cdot(\\int 6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x )\\\\ y & =(4)\\cdot(6\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right))+C \\\\  & =(4)\\cdot(2{x}^{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y=(4)\\cdot(2{x}^{3})+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(-2)=-64$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-64 & =8 (-2)^{3}+C \\\\ -64 & =-64+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny=8 x^{3}.\n$$" "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(4)\\cdot(6{x}^{2}).\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =(4)\\cdot(\\int 6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x )\\\\ y & =(4)\\cdot\\left(6\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\right)+C \\\\  & =(4)\\cdot(2{x}^{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y=(4)\\cdot(2{x}^{3})+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(-2)=-64$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-64 & =8 (-2)^{3}+C \\\\ -64 & =-64+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny=8 x^{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186642300 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{hour}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{min}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{hour}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{hour}$ with $\\text{min}.$ To get rid of $\\text{hour},$ we can put $\\text{hour}$ in the numerator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{min},$ we can put $\\text{min}$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{hour}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{hour}}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{min}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $30\\,\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{hour}}.$\n\n- There are $60$ minutes in one hour. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\frac{1}{60}$ hours in one minute.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30\\,\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{hour}} & =30\\,\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{hour}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{hour}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =30\\cdot \\frac{1}{60}(\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{hour}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{min}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{hour}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{hour}$ with $\\text{min}.$ To get rid of $\\text{hour},$ we can put $\\text{hour}$ in the numerator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{min},$ we can put $\\text{min}$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{hour}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{hour}}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{min}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $30\\,\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{hour}}.$\n\n- There are $60$ minutes in one hour. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\frac{1}{60}$ hours in one minute.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30\\,\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{hour}} & =30\\,\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{hour}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{hour}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =30\\cdot \\frac{1}{60}\\left(\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186642301 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{weeks}}{\\text{year}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{month}}.$ We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{weeks}}{\\text{year}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{month}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\text{weeks}$ to $\\text{days}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{weeks}}}{\\text{year}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{days}}{\\cancel{\\text{weeks}}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{month}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\text{year}$ into $\\text{month}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{weeks}}}{\\cancel{\\text{year}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{days}}{\\cancel{\\text{weeks}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{years}}}{\\text{month}}=\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{month}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{weeks}}{\\text{year}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{weeks}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{years}}{\\text{month}}=\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{month}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $20\\,\\frac{\\text{weeks}}{\\text{year}}.$\n\n- There are $7$ days per week.\n\n- There are $12$ months per year, therefore, there are $\\frac{1}{12}$ years per month.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20\\,\\frac{\\text{weeks}}{\\text{year}} & =20\\,\\frac{\\text{weeks}}{\\text{year}}\\cdot 7\\,\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{weeks}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}\\frac{\\text{years}}{\\text{month}} \\\\  & =20\\cdot 7\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{weeks}}}{\\cancel{\\text{year}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{days}}{\\cancel{\\text{weeks}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{years}}}{\\text{month}}) \\\\  & ≈12\\,\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{month}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThus, Daniel travels $12$ days per month." "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{weeks}}{\\text{year}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{month}}.$ We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{weeks}}{\\text{year}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{month}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\text{weeks}$ to $\\text{days}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{weeks}}}{\\text{year}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{days}}{\\cancel{\\text{weeks}}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{month}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\text{year}$ into $\\text{month}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{weeks}}}{\\cancel{\\text{year}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{days}}{\\cancel{\\text{weeks}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{years}}}{\\text{month}}=\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{month}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{weeks}}{\\text{year}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{weeks}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{years}}{\\text{month}}=\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{month}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $20\\,\\frac{\\text{weeks}}{\\text{year}}.$\n\n- There are $7$ days per week.\n\n- There are $12$ months per year, therefore, there are $\\frac{1}{12}$ years per month.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20\\,\\frac{\\text{weeks}}{\\text{year}} & =20\\,\\frac{\\text{weeks}}{\\text{year}}\\cdot 7\\,\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{weeks}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}\\frac{\\text{years}}{\\text{month}} \\\\  & =20\\cdot 7\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}\\left(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{weeks}}}{\\cancel{\\text{year}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{days}}{\\cancel{\\text{weeks}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{years}}}{\\text{month}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈12\\,\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{month}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThus, Daniel travels $12$ days per month."] [:db/cas 17592186642305 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the expression $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-x}}$ in the form $a(1+bx)^{n}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-x}} & =\\frac{2}{(2-x)^{1/2}} \\\\  & =2(2-x)^{-1/2} \\\\  & =2{(2(1-\\frac{1}{2}x))}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =2(2)^{-1/2}{(1-\\frac{1}{2}x)}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{(1-\\frac{1}{2}x)}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the series on the right-hand side of \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+n(n-1)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+n(n-1)(n-2)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+⋯\n$$\n\nconverges only when $|x|<1.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-x}}=\\sqrt{2}{(1-\\frac{1}{2}x)}^{-1/2}=\\sqrt{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}x+\\frac{3}{16\\sqrt{2}}{x}^{2}+⋯\n$$\n\nis valid only for $|-\\frac{1}{2}x|<1$ or, equivalently, for $|x|<2.$" "First, we write the expression $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-x}}$ in the form $a(1+bx)^{n}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-x}} & =\\frac{2}{(2-x)^{1/2}} \\\\  & =2(2-x)^{-1/2} \\\\  & =2{\\left(2\\left(1-\\frac{1}{2}x\\right)\\right)}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =2(2)^{-1/2}{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{2}x\\right)}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{2}x\\right)}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the series on the right-hand side of \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+n(n-1)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+n(n-1)(n-2)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+⋯\n$$\n\nconverges only when $|x|<1.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-x}}=\\sqrt{2}{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{2}x\\right)}^{-1/2}=\\sqrt{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}x+\\frac{3}{16\\sqrt{2}}{x}^{2}+⋯\n$$\n\nis valid only for $|-\\frac{1}{2}x|<1$ or, equivalently, for $|x|<2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186642306 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{km}$ with $\\text{m}.$ To get rid of $\\text{km},$ we can put $\\text{km}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{m},$ we can put $\\text{m}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The speed in the problem statement is $0.125\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ meters per kilometer.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.125\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}} & =0.125\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{km}} \\\\  & =0.125\\cdot 1\\,000(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}) \\\\  & =125\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the airplane travels at a speed of $125\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{km}$ with $\\text{m}.$ To get rid of $\\text{km},$ we can put $\\text{km}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{m},$ we can put $\\text{m}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The speed in the problem statement is $0.125\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ meters per kilometer.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.125\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}} & =0.125\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{km}} \\\\  & =0.125\\cdot 1\\,000\\left(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}\\right) \\\\  & =125\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the airplane travels at a speed of $125\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186642308 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{m}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{cm}}.$ We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{cm}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\text{kg}$ to $\\text{g}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{cm}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\text{m}$ into $\\text{cm}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\text{cm}}=\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{cm}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{kg}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}}=\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{cm}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{m}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ grams per kilogram.\n\n- There are $100$ centimeters per meter, therefore, there are $\\frac{1}{100}$ meters per centimeter.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{m}} & =5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{m}}\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{kg}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1\\,000\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\text{cm}}) \\\\  & =50\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{cm}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $5$ kilograms per meter is equivalent to $50$ grams per centimeter. One centimeter of cable weighs $50$ grams." "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{m}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{cm}}.$ We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{cm}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\text{kg}$ to $\\text{g}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{cm}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\text{m}$ into $\\text{cm}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\text{cm}}=\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{cm}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{kg}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}}=\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{cm}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{m}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ grams per kilogram.\n\n- There are $100$ centimeters per meter, therefore, there are $\\frac{1}{100}$ meters per centimeter.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{m}} & =5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{m}}\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{kg}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1\\,000\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\left(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\text{cm}}\\right) \\\\  & =50\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{cm}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $5$ kilograms per meter is equivalent to $50$ grams per centimeter. One centimeter of cable weighs $50$ grams."] [:db/cas 17592186642314 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{kg}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{g}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{kg}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{g}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{kg}$ with $\\text{g}.$ To get rid of $\\text{kg},$ we can put $\\text{kg}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{g},$ we can put $\\text{g}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}=\\frac{\\text{g}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{kg}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{kg}}=\\frac{\\text{g}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The pressure given in the problem statement is $0.0704\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ grams per kilogram.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.0704\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}} & =0.0704\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{kg}} \\\\  & =0.0704\\cdot 1\\,000(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}) \\\\  & =70.4\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the pressure of the water pump is $70.4\\,\\text{g}/{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$" "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{kg}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{g}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{kg}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{g}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{kg}$ with $\\text{g}.$ To get rid of $\\text{kg},$ we can put $\\text{kg}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{g},$ we can put $\\text{g}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}=\\frac{\\text{g}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{kg}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{kg}}=\\frac{\\text{g}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The pressure given in the problem statement is $0.0704\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ grams per kilogram.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.0704\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}} & =0.0704\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{kg}} \\\\  & =0.0704\\cdot 1\\,000\\left(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}}\\right) \\\\  & =70.4\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the pressure of the water pump is $70.4\\,\\text{g}/{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186642315 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the expression $3\\sqrt[4]{81-6x}$ in the form $a(1+bx)^{n}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt[4]{81-6x} & =3(81-6x)^{1/4} \\\\  & =3{(81(1-\\frac{2}{27}x))}^{1/4} \\\\  & =3(81)^{1/4}{(1-\\frac{2}{27}x)}^{1/4} \\\\  & =9{(1-\\frac{2}{27}x)}^{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the series on the right-hand side of \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+n(n-1)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+n(n-1)(n-2)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+⋯\n$$\n\nconverges only when $|x|<1.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n3\\sqrt[4]{81-6x}=9{(1-\\frac{2}{27}x)}^{1/4}=9-\\frac{1}{6}x-\\frac{1}{216}{x}^{2}-⋯\n$$\n\nis valid only for $|-\\frac{2}{27}x|<1$ or, equivalently, for $|x|<\\frac{27}{2}.$" "First, we write the expression $3\\sqrt[4]{81-6x}$ in the form $a(1+bx)^{n}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt[4]{81-6x} & =3(81-6x)^{1/4} \\\\  & =3{\\left(81\\left(1-\\frac{2}{27}x\\right)\\right)}^{1/4} \\\\  & =3(81)^{1/4}{\\left(1-\\frac{2}{27}x\\right)}^{1/4} \\\\  & =9{\\left(1-\\frac{2}{27}x\\right)}^{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the series on the right-hand side of \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+n(n-1)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+n(n-1)(n-2)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+⋯\n$$\n\nconverges only when $|x|<1.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n3\\sqrt[4]{81-6x}=9{\\left(1-\\frac{2}{27}x\\right)}^{1/4}=9-\\frac{1}{6}x-\\frac{1}{216}{x}^{2}-⋯\n$$\n\nis valid only for $|-\\frac{2}{27}x|<1$ or, equivalently, for $|x|<\\frac{27}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186642316 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{m}$ with $\\text{cm}.$ To get rid of $\\text{m},$ we can put $\\text{m}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{cm},$ we can put $\\text{cm}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{m}}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The standard gravity is $9.8\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$\n\n- There are $100$ centimeters per meter.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9.8\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}} & =9.8\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot 100\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{m}} \\\\  & =9.8\\cdot 100(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}) \\\\  & =980\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the earth's gravity is $980\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$" "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{m}$ with $\\text{cm}.$ To get rid of $\\text{m},$ we can put $\\text{m}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{cm},$ we can put $\\text{cm}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{m}}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The standard gravity is $9.8\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$\n\n- There are $100$ centimeters per meter.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9.8\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}} & =9.8\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot 100\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{m}} \\\\  & =9.8\\cdot 100\\left(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}\\right) \\\\  & =980\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the earth's gravity is $980\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186642318 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the expression $\\frac{1}{6-3x}$ in the form $a(1+bx)^{n}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6-3x} & =(6-3x)^{-1} \\\\  & ={(6(1-\\frac{1}{2}x))}^{-1} \\\\  & ={6}^{-1}{(1-\\frac{1}{2}x)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{(1-\\frac{1}{2}x)}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the series on the right-hand side of \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+n(n-1)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+n(n-1)(n-2)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+⋯\n$$\n\nconverges only when $|x|<1.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6-3x}=\\frac{1}{6}{(1-\\frac{1}{2}x)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{12}x+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{2}+⋯\n$$\n\nis valid only for $|-\\frac{1}{2}x|<1$ or, equivalently, for $|x|<2.$" "First, we write the expression $\\frac{1}{6-3x}$ in the form $a(1+bx)^{n}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6-3x} & =(6-3x)^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\left(6\\left(1-\\frac{1}{2}x\\right)\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & ={6}^{-1}{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{2}x\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{2}x\\right)}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the series on the right-hand side of \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+n(n-1)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+n(n-1)(n-2)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+⋯\n$$\n\nconverges only when $|x|<1.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6-3x}=\\frac{1}{6}{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{2}x\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{12}x+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{2}+⋯\n$$\n\nis valid only for $|-\\frac{1}{2}x|<1$ or, equivalently, for $|x|<2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186642322 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{hour}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{hour}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{min}$ with $\\text{hour}.$ To get rid of $\\text{min},$ we can put $\\text{min}$ in the numerator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{h},$ we can put $\\text{hour}$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{h}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{h}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{h}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{h}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $120\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}}.$\n\n- There are $60$ minutes in one hour.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}} & =120\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot 60\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{h}} \\\\  & =120\\cdot 60(\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{h}}) \\\\  & =7\\,200\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{h}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the student covers $7\\,200$ meters every hour." "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{hour}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{hour}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{min}$ with $\\text{hour}.$ To get rid of $\\text{min},$ we can put $\\text{min}$ in the numerator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{h},$ we can put $\\text{hour}$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{h}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{h}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{h}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{h}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $120\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}}.$\n\n- There are $60$ minutes in one hour.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}} & =120\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot 60\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{h}} \\\\  & =120\\cdot 60\\left(\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{h}}\\right) \\\\  & =7\\,200\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{h}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the student covers $7\\,200$ meters every hour."] [:db/cas 17592186642340 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{month}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\text{month}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{month}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\text{month}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{cm}$ with $\\text{mm}.$ To get rid of $\\text{cm},$ we can put $\\text{cm}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{mm},$ we can put $\\text{mm}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\text{month}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}=\\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\text{month}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{month}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\text{cm}}=\\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\text{month}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The growth rate given in the problem statement is $1.12\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{month}}.$\n\n- There are $10$ milimeters per centimeter.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.12\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{month}} & =1.12\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{month}}\\cdot 10\\,\\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\text{cm}} \\\\  & =1.12\\cdot 10(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\text{month}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}) \\\\  & =11.2\\,\\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\text{month}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the hair grows at a rate of $11.2\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{month}.$" "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{month}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\text{month}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{month}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\text{month}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{cm}$ with $\\text{mm}.$ To get rid of $\\text{cm},$ we can put $\\text{cm}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{mm},$ we can put $\\text{mm}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\text{month}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}=\\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\text{month}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{month}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\text{cm}}=\\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\text{month}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The growth rate given in the problem statement is $1.12\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{month}}.$\n\n- There are $10$ milimeters per centimeter.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.12\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{month}} & =1.12\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{month}}\\cdot 10\\,\\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\text{cm}} \\\\  & =1.12\\cdot 10\\left(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\text{month}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}\\right) \\\\  & =11.2\\,\\frac{\\text{mm}}{\\text{month}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the hair grows at a rate of $11.2\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{month}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186642345 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{day}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{week}}.$ We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{week}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\text{min}$ to $\\text{hours}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{week}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\text{day}$ into $\\text{week}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\cancel{\\text{day}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{days}}}{\\text{week}}=\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{week}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{week}}=\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{week}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $30\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{day}}.$\n\n- There are $60$ minutes per hour, therefore, there are $\\frac{1}{60}$ hours per minute.\n\n- There are $7$ days per week.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{day}} & =30\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot 7\\,\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{week}} \\\\  & =30\\cdot \\frac{1}{60}\\cdot 7(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\cancel{\\text{day}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{days}}}{\\text{week}}) \\\\  & =3.5\\,\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{week}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $30$ minutes per day is equivalent to $3.5$ hours per week. In one week, David walks for $3.5$ hours." "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{day}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{week}}.$ We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{week}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\text{min}$ to $\\text{hours}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{week}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\text{day}$ into $\\text{week}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\cancel{\\text{day}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{days}}}{\\text{week}}=\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{week}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{week}}=\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{week}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $30\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{day}}.$\n\n- There are $60$ minutes per hour, therefore, there are $\\frac{1}{60}$ hours per minute.\n\n- There are $7$ days per week.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{day}} & =30\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot 7\\,\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{week}} \\\\  & =30\\cdot \\frac{1}{60}\\cdot 7\\left(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\cancel{\\text{day}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{days}}}{\\text{week}}\\right) \\\\  & =3.5\\,\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{week}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $30$ minutes per day is equivalent to $3.5$ hours per week. In one week, David walks for $3.5$ hours."] [:db/cas 17592186642357 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the expression $\\frac{25}{(5+x)^{3}}$ in the form $a(1+bx)^{n}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{25}{(5+x)^{3}} & =25(5+x)^{-3} \\\\  & =25{(5(1+\\frac{1}{5}x))}^{-3} \\\\  & =25(5)^{-3}{(1+\\frac{1}{5}x)}^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{(1+\\frac{1}{5}x)}^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the series on the right-hand side of \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+n(n-1)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+n(n-1)(n-2)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+⋯\n$$\n\nconverges only when $|x|<1.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{(5+x)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{5}{(1+\\frac{1}{5}x)}^{-3}=\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{3}{25}x+\\frac{6}{125}{x}^{2}-⋯\n$$\n\nis valid only for $|\\frac{1}{5}x|<1$ or, equivalently, for $|x|<5.$" "First, we write the expression $\\frac{25}{(5+x)^{3}}$ in the form $a(1+bx)^{n}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{25}{(5+x)^{3}} & =25(5+x)^{-3} \\\\  & =25{\\left(5\\left(1+\\frac{1}{5}x\\right)\\right)}^{-3} \\\\  & =25(5)^{-3}{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{5}x\\right)}^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{5}x\\right)}^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the series on the right-hand side of \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+n(n-1)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+n(n-1)(n-2)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+⋯\n$$\n\nconverges only when $|x|<1.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{(5+x)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{5}{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{5}x\\right)}^{-3}=\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{3}{25}x+\\frac{6}{125}{x}^{2}-⋯\n$$\n\nis valid only for $|\\frac{1}{5}x|<1$ or, equivalently, for $|x|<5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186642443 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\cos \\alpha(\\frac{4\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}+\\frac{3\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}).\n$$" "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\cos \\alpha\\left(\\frac{4\\cdot \\sin \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha}+\\frac{3\\cdot \\cos \\alpha}{\\sin \\alpha}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186642521 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin t(3\\sin t+3\\frac{1}{\\sin t})+3\\cos^{2} t.$ Assume $\\sin t\\ne0$." "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin t\\left(3\\sin t+3\\frac{1}{\\sin t}\\right)+3\\cos^{2} t.$ Assume $\\sin t\\ne0$."] [:db/cas 17592186642521 :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\sin t(3\\sin t+3\\frac{1}{\\sin t}) \\\\ &\\quad {}+3\\cos^{2} t \\\\ &= 3\\sin^{2} t \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\sin t\\cdot\\frac{3}{\\sin t} \\\\ &\\quad {}+3\\cos^{2} t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 t$ and $\\cos^2 t$ terms and simplify any matching factors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& 3\\sin^{2} t \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\sin t\\cdot\\frac{3}{\\sin t} \\\\ &\\quad {}+3\\cos^{2} t \\\\ &= 3(\\sin^{2} t+\\cos^{2} t)+3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& 3(\\sin^{2} t+\\cos^{2} t)+3 \\\\ &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\sin t(3\\sin t+3\\frac{1}{\\sin t}) \\\\ &\\quad {}+3\\cos^{2} t \\\\ &= 6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\sin t\\left(3\\sin t+3\\frac{1}{\\sin t}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+3\\cos^{2} t \\\\ &= 3\\sin^{2} t \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\sin t\\cdot\\frac{3}{\\sin t} \\\\ &\\quad {}+3\\cos^{2} t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 t$ and $\\cos^2 t$ terms and simplify any matching factors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& 3\\sin^{2} t \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\sin t\\cdot\\frac{3}{\\sin t} \\\\ &\\quad {}+3\\cos^{2} t \\\\ &= 3(\\sin^{2} t+\\cos^{2} t)+3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& 3(\\sin^{2} t+\\cos^{2} t)+3 \\\\ &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\sin t\\left(3\\sin t+3\\frac{1}{\\sin t}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+3\\cos^{2} t \\\\ &= 6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186642529 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\cos x(6\\cos x-10\\frac{1}{\\cos x})+6\\sin^{2} x.$ Assume $\\cos x\\ne0$." "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\cos x\\left(6\\cos x-10\\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\right)+6\\sin^{2} x.$ Assume $\\cos x\\ne0$."] [:db/cas 17592186642529 :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\cos x(6\\cos x-10\\frac{1}{\\cos x}) \\\\ &\\quad {}+6\\sin^{2} x \\\\ &= 6\\cos^{2} x \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\cos x\\cdot\\frac{-10}{\\cos x} \\\\ &\\quad {}+6\\sin^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 x$ and $\\cos^2 x$ terms and simplify any matching factors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& 6\\cos^{2} x \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\cos x\\cdot\\frac{-10}{\\cos x} \\\\ &\\quad {}+6\\sin^{2} x \\\\ &= 6(\\cos^{2} x+\\sin^{2} x)-10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2 x+\\cos^2 x=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& 6(\\cos^{2} x+\\sin^{2} x)-10 \\\\ &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\cos x(6\\cos x-10\\frac{1}{\\cos x}) \\\\ &\\quad {}+6\\sin^{2} x \\\\ &= -4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\cos x\\left(6\\cos x-10\\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+6\\sin^{2} x \\\\ &= 6\\cos^{2} x \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\cos x\\cdot\\frac{-10}{\\cos x} \\\\ &\\quad {}+6\\sin^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 x$ and $\\cos^2 x$ terms and simplify any matching factors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& 6\\cos^{2} x \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\cos x\\cdot\\frac{-10}{\\cos x} \\\\ &\\quad {}+6\\sin^{2} x \\\\ &= 6(\\cos^{2} x+\\sin^{2} x)-10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2 x+\\cos^2 x=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& 6(\\cos^{2} x+\\sin^{2} x)-10 \\\\ &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\cos x\\left(6\\cos x-10\\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+6\\sin^{2} x \\\\ &= -4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186643123 :question/worked-solution "The generalized binomial formula states that\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1){x}^{2}}{2!}+⋯.\n$$\n\nFirst, factoring $4$ out of the binomial, we rewrite the given expression as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4+6x} & =(4+6x)^{1/2} \\\\  & ={(4(1+\\frac{3}{2}x))}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={4}^{1/2}{(1+\\frac{3}{2}x)}^{1/2} \\\\  & =2{(1+\\frac{3}{2}x)}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the generalized binomial formula with $n=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\frac{3}{2}x$ instead of $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4+6x} & =2{(1+\\frac{3}{2}x)}^{1/2} \\\\  & =2[1+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(\\frac{3}{2}x)+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(\\frac{1}{2}-1){(\\frac{3}{2}x)}^{2}}{2!}+⋯] \\\\  & =2[1+\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right){x}^{2}}{2}+⋯] \\\\  & =2[1+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{9}{32}{x}^{2}+⋯] \\\\  & =2+\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{9}{16}{x}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe binomial expansion is valid for $|\\frac{3}{2}x|<1$ or, equivalently, for $|x|<\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nSince we want to approximate $\\sqrt{5}$ using $\\sqrt{4+6x},$ we find a suitable value of $x$ by equating the expressions and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & =\\sqrt{4+6x} \\\\ 5 & =4+6x \\\\ 1 & =6x \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, since $|\\frac{1}{6}|<\\frac{2}{3},$ this value lies within the permitted range of validity.\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the value of $\\sqrt{5}$ by substituting $x=\\frac{1}{6}$ into the given expansion. Doing so, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4+6\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)} & ≈2+\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)-\\frac{9}{16}{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{4+1} & ≈2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & ≈\\frac{143}{64} \\\\  & ≈2.234,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "The generalized binomial formula states that\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1){x}^{2}}{2!}+⋯.\n$$\n\nFirst, factoring $4$ out of the binomial, we rewrite the given expression as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4+6x} & =(4+6x)^{1/2} \\\\  & ={\\left(4\\left(1+\\frac{3}{2}x\\right)\\right)}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={4}^{1/2}{\\left(1+\\frac{3}{2}x\\right)}^{1/2} \\\\  & =2{\\left(1+\\frac{3}{2}x\\right)}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the generalized binomial formula with $n=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\frac{3}{2}x$ instead of $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4+6x} & =2{\\left(1+\\frac{3}{2}x\\right)}^{1/2} \\\\  & =2\\left[1+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{2}x\\right)+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-1\\right){\\left(\\frac{3}{2}x\\right)}^{2}}{2!}+⋯\\right] \\\\  & =2\\left[1+\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right){x}^{2}}{2}+⋯\\right] \\\\  & =2\\left[1+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{9}{32}{x}^{2}+⋯\\right] \\\\  & =2+\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{9}{16}{x}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe binomial expansion is valid for $|\\frac{3}{2}x|<1$ or, equivalently, for $|x|<\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nSince we want to approximate $\\sqrt{5}$ using $\\sqrt{4+6x},$ we find a suitable value of $x$ by equating the expressions and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & =\\sqrt{4+6x} \\\\ 5 & =4+6x \\\\ 1 & =6x \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, since $|\\frac{1}{6}|<\\frac{2}{3},$ this value lies within the permitted range of validity.\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the value of $\\sqrt{5}$ by substituting $x=\\frac{1}{6}$ into the given expansion. Doing so, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4+6\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)} & ≈2+\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)-\\frac{9}{16}{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{4+1} & ≈2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & ≈\\frac{143}{64} \\\\  & ≈2.234,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186643138 :question/worked-solution "The generalized binomial formula states that\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1){x}^{2}}{2!}+⋯.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the given expression as\n\n$$\n8\\sqrt{1-2x}=8(1-2x)^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the generalized binomial formula with $n=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $-2x$ instead of $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{1-2x} & =8(1-2x)^{1/2} \\\\  & =8[1+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(-2x)+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(\\frac{1}{2}-1)(-2{x}^{2})}{2!}+⋯] \\\\  & =8[1-x+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)4{x}^{2}}{2}+⋯] \\\\  & =8[1-x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+⋯] \\\\  & =8-8x-4{x}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe binomial expansion is valid for $|-2x|<1$ or, equivalently, for $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSince we want to approximate $\\sqrt{80}$ using $8\\sqrt{1-2x},$ we find a suitable value of $x$ by equating the expressions and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{1-2x} & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\ 64(1-2x) & =80 \\\\ 1-2x & =1.25 \\\\ 2x & =-0.25 \\\\ x & =-0.125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, since $|-0.125|<\\frac{1}{2},$ this value lies within the permitted range of validity.\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the value of $\\sqrt{80}$ by substituting $x=-0.125$ into the given expansion. Doing so, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{1-2(-0.125)} & ≈8-8(-0.125)-4(-0.125)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{8}^{2}\\cdot (1+0.25)} & ≈8+1-0.0625 \\\\ \\sqrt{80} & ≈8.938,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "The generalized binomial formula states that\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1){x}^{2}}{2!}+⋯.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the given expression as\n\n$$\n8\\sqrt{1-2x}=8(1-2x)^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the generalized binomial formula with $n=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $-2x$ instead of $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{1-2x} & =8(1-2x)^{1/2} \\\\  & =8\\left[1+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(-2x)+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-1\\right)(-2{x}^{2})}{2!}+⋯\\right] \\\\  & =8\\left[1-x+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)4{x}^{2}}{2}+⋯\\right] \\\\  & =8\\left[1-x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+⋯\\right] \\\\  & =8-8x-4{x}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe binomial expansion is valid for $|-2x|<1$ or, equivalently, for $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSince we want to approximate $\\sqrt{80}$ using $8\\sqrt{1-2x},$ we find a suitable value of $x$ by equating the expressions and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{1-2x} & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\ 64(1-2x) & =80 \\\\ 1-2x & =1.25 \\\\ 2x & =-0.25 \\\\ x & =-0.125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, since $|-0.125|<\\frac{1}{2},$ this value lies within the permitted range of validity.\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the value of $\\sqrt{80}$ by substituting $x=-0.125$ into the given expansion. Doing so, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{1-2(-0.125)} & ≈8-8(-0.125)-4(-0.125)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{8}^{2}\\cdot (1+0.25)} & ≈8+1-0.0625 \\\\ \\sqrt{80} & ≈8.938,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186643162 :question/worked-solution "The generalized binomial formula states that\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1){x}^{2}}{2!}+⋯.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the given expression as\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{1-8x}=(1-8x)^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the generalized binomial formula with $n=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $-8x$ instead of $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1-8x} & =(1-8x)^{1/2} \\\\  & =1+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(-8x)+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(\\frac{1}{2}-1)(-8x)^{2}}{2!}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-4x+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(64){x}^{2}}{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-4x-8{x}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe binomial expansion is valid for $|8x|<1$ or, equivalently, for $|x|<\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nNote that, since $|-0.025|<\\frac{1}{8},$ this value lies within the permitted range of validity.\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the value of $\\sqrt{30}$ by substituting $x=-0.025$ into the given expansion. Doing so, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1-8(-0.025)} & ≈1-4(-0.025)-8(-0.025)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{1.2} & ≈1+0.1-0.005 \\\\ \\sqrt{1.2\\cdot \\frac{30}{30}} & ≈1.095 \\\\ \\sqrt{0.04\\cdot 30} & ≈1.095 \\\\ 0.2\\sqrt{30} & ≈1.095 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{30} & ≈1.095 \\\\ \\sqrt{30} & ≈5\\cdot 1.095 \\\\  & ≈5.475.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The generalized binomial formula states that\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1){x}^{2}}{2!}+⋯.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the given expression as\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{1-8x}=(1-8x)^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the generalized binomial formula with $n=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $-8x$ instead of $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1-8x} & =(1-8x)^{1/2} \\\\  & =1+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(-8x)+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-1\\right)(-8x)^{2}}{2!}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-4x+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(64){x}^{2}}{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-4x-8{x}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe binomial expansion is valid for $|8x|<1$ or, equivalently, for $|x|<\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nNote that, since $|-0.025|<\\frac{1}{8},$ this value lies within the permitted range of validity.\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the value of $\\sqrt{30}$ by substituting $x=-0.025$ into the given expansion. Doing so, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1-8(-0.025)} & ≈1-4(-0.025)-8(-0.025)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{1.2} & ≈1+0.1-0.005 \\\\ \\sqrt{1.2\\cdot \\frac{30}{30}} & ≈1.095 \\\\ \\sqrt{0.04\\cdot 30} & ≈1.095 \\\\ 0.2\\sqrt{30} & ≈1.095 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{30} & ≈1.095 \\\\ \\sqrt{30} & ≈5\\cdot 1.095 \\\\  & ≈5.475.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186643163 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((3x-2y)+(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(3\\cdot 0+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12} \\\\  & ≈0.58.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=0.58.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{2}{3}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (0.58)) \\\\  & ≈0.94.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈0.58,\\,y(1)≈0.94.\n$$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((3x-2y)+(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\left(3\\cdot 0+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+2\\cdot 1\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12} \\\\  & ≈0.58.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=0.58.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\left(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{2}{3}y\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\left(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (0.58)\\right) \\\\  & ≈0.94.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈0.58,\\,y(1)≈0.94.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186643665 :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\frac{10}{3})" "\\left(-\\infty ,\\frac{10}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186643852 :answer/value "[-1,-\\frac{7}{2}]" "\\left[-1,-\\frac{7}{2}\\right]"] [:db/cas 17592186643963 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right)]" "\\frac{1}{15}\\left[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right)\\right]"] [:db/cas 17592186644078 :question/problem "Simplify the expression $$\n\\displaystyle {[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right)]}^{15}.\n$$" "Simplify the expression $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right)\\right]}^{15}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186644078 :question/worked-solution "For expressions involving subtraction, De Moivre's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=15$ and $\\phi =\\frac{3\\theta }{5}.$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right)]}^{15} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(15\\cdot \\frac{3\\theta }{5})-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(15\\cdot \\frac{3\\theta }{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For expressions involving subtraction, De Moivre's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=15$ and $\\phi =\\frac{3\\theta }{5}.$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right)\\right]}^{15} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(15\\cdot \\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(15\\cdot \\frac{3\\theta }{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186644082 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) &=\\frac{3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=\\frac{1}{e},e$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{e}\\right) & =\\frac{3{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{e}\\right))}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{e}\\right)}=3e>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{2}}{e}=\\frac{3}{e}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{1}{e}$ |  | $e$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) &=\\frac{3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=\\frac{1}{e},e$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{e}\\right) & =\\frac{3{\\left(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{e}\\right)\\right)}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{e}\\right)}=3e>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{2}}{e}=\\frac{3}{e}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{1}{e}$ |  | $e$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema."] [:db/cas 17592186644093 :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=36,\\,\\mu =10,\\,\\sigma =18.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(10,\\frac{{18}^{2}}{36}) \\\\  & ∼N(10,9).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=36,\\,\\mu =10,\\,\\sigma =18.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,\\left(10,\\frac{{18}^{2}}{36}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(10,9).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186644111 :question/worked-solution "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}y{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}y[\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{8}y+\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}-\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}y{\\left[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}y\\left[\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\right]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{3}{8}y+\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{4}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}-\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186644239 :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-2,4)$ and $Y(-6,4),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{((-2)-(-6))^2+(4-4)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^2+(0)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{-2+(-6)}{2},\\frac{4+4}{2})=(-4,4)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=2$ and center $(a,b)=(-4,4),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-(-4))^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =(2)^2 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-2,4)$ and $Y(-6,4),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{((-2)-(-6))^2+(4-4)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^2+(0)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{-2+(-6)}{2},\\frac{4+4}{2}\\right)=(-4,4)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=2$ and center $(a,b)=(-4,4),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-(-4))^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =(2)^2 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186644243 :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(3,6)$ and $Y(3,2),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(3-3)^2+(6-2)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(4)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a 