aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-40.5+(17.4)+(-2)=-25.1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186663691 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0.1)≈0.91" "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0.1\\right)≈0.91"] [:db/cas 17592186663698 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0.1)≈0.89" "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0.1\\right)≈0.89"] [:db/cas 17592186663749 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0.1)≈0.96" "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0.1\\right)≈0.96"] [:db/cas 17592186663766 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0.1)≈0.82" "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0.1\\right)≈0.82"] [:db/cas 17592186663790 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0.1)≈0.78" "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0.1\\right)≈0.78"] [:db/cas 17592186664041 :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $Y∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P(Z\\ge \\frac{y-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =-1$ and $\\sigma =2.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge -4.6) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{-4.6-(-1)}{2}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge -1.8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/0f/0f6e35516f2fca412a9928a28e426b97d1812257846e4034d6776711744e2cfe.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -1.8) & =1-P(Z<-1.8) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -1.8) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-1.8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.8)=0.0359.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -1.8) & =1-Φ(-1.8) \\\\  & =1-0.0359 \\\\  & =0.9641.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge -4.6)=0.9641.\n$$" "Given a normal random variable $Y∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P\\left(Z\\ge \\frac{y