676705 :question/worked-solution "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 81=81(1+(\\frac{1}{81})x^{2})\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{x}{9}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{1}{9},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(9)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{8}{x^{2} + 81}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{8}{81}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(\\frac{x}{9})^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{8}{81}(9)\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{8}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{8 \\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{8 \\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(\\frac{x}{9} \\right)}}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 81=81\\left(1+\\left(\\frac{1}{81}\\right)x^{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{x}{9}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{1}{9},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(9)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{8}{x^{2} + 81}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{8}{81}\\int \\frac{1}{1+\\left(\\frac{x}{9}\\right)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{8}{81}(9)\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{8}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{8 \\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{8 \\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(\\frac{x}{9} \\right)}}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186676713 :question/worked-solution "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\n252 x^{2} + 7=7(1+(36)x^{2})\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=6 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mat