rname{arctan}{\\left(8 x \\right)}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186676795 :question/worked-solution "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 4=4(1+(\\frac{1}{4})x^{2})\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(2)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{8}{x^{2} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 2\\int \\frac{1}{1+(\\frac{x}{2})^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 2(2)\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 4\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 4 \\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 4 \\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 4=4\\left(1+\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)x^{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(2)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{8}{x^{2} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 2\\int \\frac{1}{1+\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 2(2)\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 4\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 4 \\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 4 \\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186676807 :question/worked-solution "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 9=9(1+(\\frac{1}{9})x^{2})\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3},\\qquad