6676869 :question/worked-solution "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\n8 x^{2} + 2=2(1+(4)x^{2})\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=2 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{1}{8 x^{2} + 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(2 x)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2})\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\n8 x^{2} + 2=2(1+(4)x^{2})\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=2 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{1}{8 x^{2} + 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(2 x)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186676884 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=9 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=9,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{9})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|9 x|=9|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{9}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{9}{|9 x|\\sqrt{(9 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9\\int \\frac{1}{|9 x|\\sqrt{(9 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9(\\frac{1}{9})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(9 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|9 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=9 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=9,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|9 x|=9|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{9}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{9}{|9 x|\\sqrt{(9 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9\\int \\frac{1}{|9 x|\\sqrt{(9 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(9 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|9 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186676889 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=8 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=8,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{8})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|8 x|=8|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{8}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{1}{|8 x|\\sqrt{(8 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|8 x|\\sqrt{(8 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1(\\frac{1}{8})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{8}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}}{8}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(8 x \\right)}}{8}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|8 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=8 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=8,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|8 x|=8|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{8}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{1}{|8 x|\\sqrt{(8 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|8 x|\\sqrt{(8 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{8}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}}{8}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(8 x \\right)}}{8}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|8 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186676900 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=14 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=14,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{14})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|14 x|=14|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{14}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{14}{|14 x|\\sqrt{(14 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 14\\int \\frac{1}{|14 x|\\sqrt{(14 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 14(\\frac{1}{14})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(14 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|14 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=14 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=14,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{14}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|14 x|=14|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{14}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{14}{|14 x|\\sqrt{(14 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 14\\int \\frac{1}{|14 x|\\sqrt{(14 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 14\\left(\\frac{1}{14}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(14 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|14 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/c