 on either interval where $|11 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186676905 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=9 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=9,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{9})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|9 x|=9|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{9}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{1}{|9 x|\\sqrt{(9 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|9 x|\\sqrt{(9 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1(\\frac{1}{9})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(9 x \\right)}}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|9 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=9 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=9,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|9 x|=9|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{9}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{1}{|9 x|\\sqrt{(9 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|9 x|\\sqrt{(9 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(9 x \\right)}}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|9 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186676917 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=6 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=6,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{6})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|6 x|=6|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{6}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{36 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 30\\int \\frac{1}{|6 x|\\sqrt{(6 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 30(\\frac{1}{6})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 5\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 5 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 5 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(6 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|6 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=6 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=6,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|6 x|=6|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{6}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{36 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 30\\int \\frac{1}{|6 x|\\sqrt{(6 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 30\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 5\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 5 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 5 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(6 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|6 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186676920 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=8 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=8,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{8})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|8 x|=8|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{8}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{9}{|8 x|\\sqrt{(8 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9\\int \\frac{1}{|8 x|\\sqrt{(8 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9(\\frac{1}{8})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{9}{8}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{9 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}}{8}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{9 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(8 x \\right)}}{8}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|8 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=8 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=8,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|8 x|=8|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{8}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{9}{|8 x|\\sqrt{(8 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9\\int \\frac{1}{|8 x|\\sqrt{(8 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{9}{8}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{9 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}}{8}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{9 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(8 x \\right)}}{8}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|8 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186676931 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=7 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=7,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{7})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|7 x|=7|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{7}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{1}{|7 x|\\sqrt{(7 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|7 x|\\sqrt{(7 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1(\\frac{1}{7})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}}{7}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(7 x \\right)}}{7}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|7 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=7 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=7,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|7 x|=7|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{7}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{1}{|7 x|\\sqrt{(7 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|7 x|\\sqrt{(7 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}}{7}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(7 x \\right)}}{7}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|7 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186676949 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=4 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|4 x|=4|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{4}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{4}{|4 x|\\sqrt{(4 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 4\\int \\frac{1}{|4 x|\\sqrt{(4 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 4(\\frac{1}{4})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(4 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|4 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=4 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|4 x|=4|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{4}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{4}{|4 x|\\sqrt{(4 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 4\\int \\frac{1}{|4 x|\\sqrt{(4 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 4\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(4 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|4 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186676960 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=3 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=3,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|3 x|=3|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{3}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{3}{|x|\\sqrt{9 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9\\int \\frac{1}{|3 x|\\sqrt{(3 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9(\\frac{1}{3})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 3\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 3 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 3 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(3 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|3 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=3 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=3,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|3 x|=3|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{3}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{3}{|x|\\sqrt{9 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9\\int \\frac{1}{|3 x|\\sqrt{(3 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 3\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 3 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 3 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(3 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|3 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186676964 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=13 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=13,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{13})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|13 x|=13|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{13}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{13}{|13 x|\\sqrt{(13 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 13\\int \\frac{1}{|13 x|\\sqrt{(13 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 13(\\frac{1}{13})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(13 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|13 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=13 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=13,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{13}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|13 x|=13|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{13}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{13}{|13 x|\\sqrt{(13 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 13\\int \\frac{1}{|13 x|\\sqrt{(13 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 13\\left(\\frac{1}{13}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(13 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|13 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186676965 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=2 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|2 x|=2|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{2}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{8}{|2 x|\\sqrt{(2 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 8\\int \\frac{1}{|2 x|\\sqrt{(2 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 8(\\frac{1}{2})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 4\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 4 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 4 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(2 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|2 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=2 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|2 x|=2|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{2}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{8}{|2 x|\\sqrt{(2 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 8\\int \\frac{1}{|2 x|\\sqrt{(2 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 8\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 4\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 4 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 4 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(2 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|2 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186676967 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=\\sqrt{3} x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\sqrt{3},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|\\sqrt{3} x|=\\sqrt{3}|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{9}{|x|\\sqrt{3 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9 \\sqrt{3}\\int \\frac{1}{|\\sqrt{3} x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3} x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9 \\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 9\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 9 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 9 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(\\sqrt{3} x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|\\sqrt{3} x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=\\sqrt{3} x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\sqrt{3},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|\\sqrt{3} x|=\\sqrt{3}|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{9}{|x|\\sqrt{3 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9 \\sqrt{3}\\int \\frac{1}{|\\sqrt{3} x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3} x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9 \\sqrt{3}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 9\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 9 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 9 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(\\sqrt{3} x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|\\sqrt{3} x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186676971 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=10 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=10,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{10})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|10 x|=10|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{10}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{10}{|10 x|\\sqrt{(10 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 10\\int \\frac{1}{|10 x|\\sqrt{(10 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 10(\\frac{1}{10})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(10 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|10 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=10 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=10,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|10 x|=10|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{10}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{10}{|10 x|\\sqrt{(10 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 10\\int \\frac{1}{|10 x|\\sqrt{(10 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 10\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(10 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|10 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186676973 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=4 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|4 x|=4|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{4}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{7}{|x|\\sqrt{16 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 28\\int \\frac{1}{|4 x|\\sqrt{(4 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 28(\\frac{1}{4})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 7\\int \\frac{1}{|u|