(\\frac{1}{7})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}}{6}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(7 x \\right)}}{6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|7 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=7 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=7,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|7 x|=7|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{7}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{1}{6|x|\\sqrt{49 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{7}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{|7 x|\\sqrt{(7 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{7}{6}\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}}{6}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(7 x \\right)}}{6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|7 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186676979 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=6 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=6,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{6})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|6 x|=6|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{6}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{6}{|6 x|\\sqrt{(6 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 6\\int \\frac{1}{|6 x|\\sqrt{(6 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 6(\\frac{1}{6})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(6 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|6 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=6 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=6,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|6 x|=6|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{6}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{6}{|6 x|\\sqrt{(6 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 6\\int \\frac{1}{|6 x|\\sqrt{(6 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 6\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(6 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|6 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186676982 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=2 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|2 x|=2|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{2}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{1}{2|x|\\sqrt{4 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|2 x|\\sqrt{(2 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1(\\frac{1}{2})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}}{2}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|2 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=2 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|2 x|=2|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{2}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{1}{2|x|\\sqrt{4 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|2 x|\\sqrt{(2 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}}{2}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|2 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186676986 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=4 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|4 x|=4|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{4}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{8}{|4 x|\\sqrt{(4 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 8\\int \\frac{1}{|4 x|\\sqrt{(4 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 8(\\frac{1}{4})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 2\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 2 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 2 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(4 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|4 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=4 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|4 x|=4|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{4}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{8}{|4 x|\\sqrt{(4 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 8\\int \\frac{1}{|4 x|\\sqrt{(4 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 8\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 2\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 2 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 2 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(4 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|4 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186676997 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=\\sqrt{7} x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\sqrt{7},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|\\sqrt{7} x|=\\sqrt{7}|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{7 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 5 \\sqrt{7}\\int \\frac{1}{|\\sqrt{7} x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{7} x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 5 \\sqrt{7}(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 5\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 5 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 5 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(\\sqrt{7} x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|\\sqrt{7} x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=\\sqrt{7} x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\sqrt{7},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|\\sqrt{7} x|=\\sqrt{7}|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{7 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 5 \\sqrt{7}\\int \\frac{1}{|\\sqrt{7} x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{7} x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 5 \\sqrt{7}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 5\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 5 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 5 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(\\sqrt{7} x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|\\sqrt{7} x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186677001 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=5 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=5,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|5 x|=5|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{5}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{20}{|5 x|\\sqrt{(5 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 20\\int \\frac{1}{|5 x|\\sqrt{(5 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 20(\\frac{1}{5})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 4\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 4 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 4 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(5 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|5 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=5 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=5,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|5 x|=5|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{5}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{20}{|5 x|\\sqrt{(5 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 20\\int \\frac{1}{|5 x|\\sqrt{(5 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 20\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 4\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 4 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 4 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(5 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|5 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186677008 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=\\sqrt{7} x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\sqrt{7},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|\\sqrt{7} x|=\\sqrt{7}|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{9}{|x|\\sqrt{7 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9 \\sqrt{7}\\int \\frac{1}{|\\sqrt{7} x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{7} x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9 \\sqrt{7}(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 9\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 9 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 9 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(\\sqrt{7} x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|\\sqrt{7} x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=\\sqrt{7} x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\sqrt{7},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|\\sqrt{7} x|=\\sqrt{7}|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{9}{|x|\\sqrt{7 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9 \\sqrt{7}\\int \\frac{1}{|\\sqrt{7} x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{7} x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9 \\sqrt{7}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 9\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 9 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 9 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(\\sqrt{7} x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|\\sqrt{7} x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186677022 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=7 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=7,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{7})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|7 x|=7|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{7}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{8}{|7 x|\\sqrt{(7 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 8\\int \\frac{1}{|7 x|\\sqrt{(7 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 8(\\frac{1}{7})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{8}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{8 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}}{7}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{8 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(7 x \\right)}}{7}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|7 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=7 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=7,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|7 x|=7|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{7}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{8}{|7 x|\\sqrt{(7 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 8\\int \\frac{1}{|7 x|\\sqrt{(7 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 8\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{8}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{8 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}}{7}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{8 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(7 x \\right)}}{7}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|7 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186677040 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=15 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=15,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{15})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|15 x|=15|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{15}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{15}{|15 x|\\sqrt{(15 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 15\\int \\frac{1}{|15 x|\\sqrt{(15 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 15(\\frac{1}{15})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(15 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|15 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals." "Use the linear substitution $u=15 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=15,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{15}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|15 x|=15|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{15}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{15}{|15 x|\\sqrt{(15 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 15\\int \\frac{1}{|15 x|\\sqrt{(15 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 15\\left(\\frac{1}{15}\\right)\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(15 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|15 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals."] [:db/cas 17592186677043 :question/worked-solution "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\n10 x^{2} + 9=9(1+(\\frac{10}{9})x^{2})\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{\\sqrt{10} x}{3}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{10}}{10})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{10}}{10}&\\Longrightarrow u=(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3})(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{10}}{10})=1 \\\\ x=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{30}}{10}&\\Longrightarrow u=(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3})(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{30}}{10})=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{\\frac{3 \\sqrt{10}}{10}}^{\\frac{3 \\sqrt{30}}{10}} \\frac{1}{10 x^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{9}\\int_{\\frac{3 \\sqrt{10}}{10}}^{\\frac{3 \\sqrt{30}}{10}} \\frac{1}{1+(\\frac{\\sqrt{10} x}{3})^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{10}}{10})\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{30}\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{30}[\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{30}(\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}(1)) \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{30}((\\frac{\\pi}{3})-(\\frac{\\pi}{4})) \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{10} \\pi}{360}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\n10 x^{2} + 9=9\\left(1+\\left(\\frac{10}{9}\\right)x^{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{\\sqrt{10} x}{3}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{10}}{10}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{10}}{10}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{10}}{10}\\right)=1 \\\\ x=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{30}}{10}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{30}}{10}\\right)=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{\\frac{3 \\sqrt{10}}{10}}^{\\frac{3 \\sqrt{30}}{10}} \\frac{1}{10 x^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{9}\\int_{\\frac{3 \\sqrt{10}}{10}}^{\\frac{3 \\sqrt{30}}{10}} \\frac{1}{1+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{10} x}{3}\\right)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{9}\\left(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{10}}{10}\\right)\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{30}\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{30}[\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{30}(\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}(1)) \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{30}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right) \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{10} \\pi}{360}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186677059 :question/worked-solution "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 10=10(1+(\\frac{1}{10})x^{2})\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{\\sqrt{10} x}{10}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\sqrt{10})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10})(0)=0 \\\\ x=\\sqrt{10}&\\Longrightarrow u=(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10})(\\sqrt{10})=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{10}} \\frac{8}{x^{2} + 10}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{4}{5}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{10}} \\frac{1}{1+(\\frac{\\sqrt{10} x}{10})^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{4}{5}(\\sqrt{10})\\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{4 \\sqrt{10}}{5}\\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{4 \\sqrt{10}}{5}[\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &= \\frac{4 \\sqrt{10}}{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}(0)) \\\\  &= \\frac{4 \\sqrt{10}}{5}((\\frac{\\pi}{4})-(0)) \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{10} \\pi}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 10=10\\left(1+\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)x^{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{\\sqrt{10} x}{10}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\sqrt{10})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\right)(0)=0 \\\\ x=\\sqrt{10}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\right)(\\sqrt{10})=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{10}} \\frac{8}{x^{2} + 10}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{4}{5}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{10}} \\frac{1}{1+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{10} x}{10}\\right)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{4}{5}(\\sqrt{10})\\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{4 \\sqrt{10}}{5}\\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{4 \\sqrt{10}}{5}[\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &= \\frac{4 \\sqrt{10}}{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}(0)) \\\\  &= \\frac{4 \\sqrt{10}}{5}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-(0)\\right) \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{10} \\pi}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186677063 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=7 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=7,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{7})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=(7)(0)=0 \\\\ x=\\frac{1}{14}&\\Longrightarrow u=(7)(\\frac{1}{14})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{14}} \\frac{6}{49 x^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 6\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{14}} \\frac{1}{1+(7 x)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 6(\\frac{1}{7})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{6}{7}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{6}{7}[\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  &= \\frac{6}{7}(\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arctan}(0)) \\\\  &= \\frac{6}{7}((\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\right)})-(0)) \\\\  &= \\frac{6 \\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\right)}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Use the linear substitution $u=7 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=7,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=(7)(0)=0 \\\\ x=\\frac{1}{14}&\\Longrightarrow u=(7)\\left(\\frac{1}{14}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{14}} \\frac{6}{49 x^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 6\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{14}} \\frac{1}{1+(7 x)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 6\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{6}{7}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{6}{7}[\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  &= \\frac{6}{7}\\left(\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}(0)\\right) \\\\  &= \\frac{6}{7}\\left(\\left(\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\right)}\\right)-(0)\\right) \\\\  &= \\frac{6 \\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\right)}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186677064 :question/worked-solution "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{16 - 6 x^{2}}=\\sqrt{16(1-(\\frac{3}{8})x^{2})}\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{\\sqrt{6} x}{4}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{6}}{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}&\\Longrightarrow u=(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3})=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}&\\Longrightarrow u=(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4})(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}}^{\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{16 - 6 x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}}^{\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(\\frac{\\sqrt{6} x}{4})^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{6}}{3})\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}[\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}(\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}((\\frac{\\pi}{4})-(\\frac{\\pi}{6})) \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{6} \\pi}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{16 - 6 x^{2}}=\\sqrt{16\\left(1-\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)x^{2}\\right)}\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{\\sqrt{6} x}{4}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{6}}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$