ame{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{\\frac{5 \\sqrt{21}}{21}}^{\\frac{5 \\sqrt{7}}{7}} \\frac{9}{7 x^{2} + 25}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{9}{25}\\int_{\\frac{5 \\sqrt{21}}{21}}^{\\frac{5 \\sqrt{7}}{7}} \\frac{1}{1+(\\frac{\\sqrt{7} x}{5})^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{9}{25}(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{7}}{7})\\int_{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{9 \\sqrt{7}}{35}\\int_{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{9 \\sqrt{7}}{35}[\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\\\  &= \\frac{9 \\sqrt{7}}{35}(\\operatorname{arctan}(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})) \\\\  &= \\frac{9 \\sqrt{7}}{35}((\\frac{\\pi}{4})-(\\frac{\\pi}{6})) \\\\  &= \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7} \\pi}{140}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\n7 x^{2} + 25=25\\left(1+\\left(\\frac{7}{25}\\right)x^{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{\\sqrt{7} x}{5}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{7}}{7}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{21}}{21}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{21}}{21}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ x=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{7}}{7}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{7}}{7}\\right)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{\\frac{5 \\sqrt{21}}{21}}^{\\frac{5 \\sqrt{7}}{7}} \\frac{9}{7 x^{2} + 25}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{9}{25}\\int_{\\frac{5 \\sqrt{21}}{21}}^{\\frac{5 \\sqrt{7}}{7}} \\frac{1}{1+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7} x}{5}\\right)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{9}{25}\\left(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{7}}{7}\\right)\\int_{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{9 \\sqrt{7}}{35}\\int_{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{9 \\sqrt{7}}{35}[\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\\\  &= \\frac{9 \\sqrt{7}}{35}\\left(\\operatorname{arctan}(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  &= \\frac{9 \\sqrt{7}}{35}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right) \\\\  &= \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7} \\pi}{140}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186677091 :question/worked-solution "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{10 - x^{2}}=\\sqrt{10(1-(\\frac{1}{10})x^{2})}\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{\\sqrt{10} x}{10}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\sqrt{10})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10})(0)=0 \\\\ x=\\sqrt{5}&\\Longrightarrow u=(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10})(\\sqrt{5})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}} \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10 - x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{10}}{5}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(\\frac{\\sqrt{10} x}{10})^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{10}}{5}(\\sqrt{10})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 4\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 4[\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{0}^{