ac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(3 \\sqrt{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=- 3 \\sqrt{3}&\\Longrightarrow u=(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9})(- 3 \\sqrt{3})=-1 \\\\ x=9&\\Longrightarrow u=(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9})(9)=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{- 3 \\sqrt{3}}^{9} \\frac{6}{3 x^{2} + 81}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{2}{27}\\int_{- 3 \\sqrt{3}}^{9} \\frac{1}{1+(\\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{9})^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{2}{27}(3 \\sqrt{3})\\int_{-1}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{9}\\int_{-1}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{9}[\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{-1}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  &= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{9}(\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}(-1)) \\\\  &= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{9}((\\frac{\\pi}{3})-(- \\frac{\\pi}{4})) \\\\  &= \\frac{7 \\sqrt{3} \\pi}{54}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\n3 x^{2} + 81=81\\left(1+\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)x^{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{9}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(3 \\sqrt{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=- 3 \\sqrt{3}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9}\\right)(- 3 \\sqrt{3})=-1 \\\\ x=9&\\Longrightarrow u=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9}\\right)(9)=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{- 3 \\sqrt{3}}^{9} \\frac{