]_{0}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\  &= 1(\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}(0)) \\\\  &= 1((\\frac{\\pi}{4})-(0)) \\\\  &= \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{25 - x^{2}}=\\sqrt{25\\left(1-\\left(\\frac{1}{25}\\right)x^{2}\\right)}\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(5)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)(0)=0 \\\\ x=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{0}^{\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25 - x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{5}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{5}(5)\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1[\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{0}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\  &= 1\\left(\\operatorname{arcsin}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arcsin}(0)\\right) \\\\  &= 1\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-(0)\\right) \\\\  &= \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186677102 :question/worked-solution "Use the linear substitution $u=8 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=8,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{8})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=(8)(0)=0 \\\\ x=\\frac{1}{8}&\\Longrightarrow u=(8)(\\frac{1}{8})=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{8}} \\frac{9}{64 x^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{8}} \\frac{1}{1+(8 x)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9(\\frac{1}{8})\\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{9}{8}\\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{9}{8}[\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &= \\frac{9}{8}(\\operatorname{arctan}(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}(0)) \\\\  &= \\frac{9}{8}((\\frac{\\pi}{4})-(0)) \\\\  &= \\frac{9 \\pi}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Use the linear substitution $u=8 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=8,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=(8)(0)=0 \\\\ x=\\frac{1}{8}&\\Longrightarrow u=(8)\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{8}} \\frac{9}{64 x^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{8}} \\frac{1}{1+(8 x)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)\\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{9}{8}\\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{9}{8}[\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &= \\frac{9}{8}(\\operatorname{arctan}(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}(0)) \\\\  &= \\frac{9}{8}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-(0)\\right) \\\\  &= \\frac{9 \\pi}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 1759218667