{3}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(\\frac{\\sqrt{6} x}{6})^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{5}{6}(\\sqrt{6})\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{5 \\sqrt{6}}{6}\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{5 \\sqrt{6}}{6}[\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\\\  &= \\frac{5 \\sqrt{6}}{6}(\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  &= \\frac{5 \\sqrt{6}}{6}((\\frac{\\pi}{4})-(\\frac{\\pi}{6})) \\\\  &= \\frac{5 \\sqrt{6} \\pi}{72}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{36 - 6 x^{2}}=\\sqrt{36\\left(1-\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)x^{2}\\right)}\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{\\sqrt{6} x}{6}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\sqrt{6})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x=\\sqrt{3}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\right)(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{36 - 6 x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{5}{6}\\int_{\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6} x}{6}\\right)^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{5}{6}(\\sqrt{6})\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{5 \\sqrt{