u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{9}} \\frac{9}{81 x^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{9}} \\frac{1}{1+(9 x)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right)\\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1\\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 1[\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &= 1(\\operatorname{arctan}(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}(0)) \\\\  &= 1\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-(0)\\right) \\\\  &= \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186677142 :question/worked-solution "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{49 - x^{2}}=\\sqrt{49(1-(\\frac{1}{49})x^{2})}\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{x}{7}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{1}{7},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(7)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=- \\frac{7}{2}&\\Longrightarrow u=(\\frac{1}{7})(- \\frac{7}{2})=- \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x=\\frac{7}{2}&\\Longrightarrow u=(\\frac{1}{7})(\\frac{7}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth transformed endpoints lie strictly between $-1$ and $1$, so the integrand is continuous throughout the interval.\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{- \\frac{7}{2}}^{\\frac{7}{2}} \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{49 - x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{6}{7}\\int_{- \\frac{7}{2}}^{\\frac{7}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(\\frac{x}{7})^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{6}{7}(7)\\int_{- \\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 6\\int_{- \\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 6[\\operatorname{arcsin}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{- \\frac{1}{2}}^{\\