$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{8}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{8}{3}&\\Longrightarrow u=(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8})(\\frac{8}{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ x=\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}&\\Longrightarrow u=(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8})(\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3})=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{\\frac{8}{3}}^{\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\frac{3}{3 x^{2} + 64}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{3}{64}\\int_{\\frac{8}{3}}^{\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\frac{1}{1+(\\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{8})^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= \\frac{3}{64}(\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\int_{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}\\int_{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}[\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}]_{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}}^{1} \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}(\\operatorname{arctan}(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})) \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}((\\frac{\\pi}{4})-(\\frac{\\pi}{6})) \\\\  &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\pi}{96}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First factor the constant in the denominator:\n\n$$\n3 x^{2} + 64=64\\left(1+\\left(\\frac{3}{64}\\right)x^{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nUse the linear substitution $u=\\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{8}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=\\left(\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nChange both bounds using the same substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{8}{3}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}\\right)\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ x=\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\left(\